    "language": "en",
    "md5": "daf9b94fc18857276de1cdc05d09c293",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1626,
    "chunk_text": "Veteran legal expert Dr. Nihal Jayawickrama who served then as the Permanent Secretary to the Ministry of Justice argues that there are no prisoners on death row now according to a procedure prescribed in a justice ministry standing order introduced when Mr. Felix Dias Bandaranaike was Minister of Justice. \u201cIf either the trial Judge or the Attorney General had recommended that the sentence should not be carried out, the Minister advised that the sentence be commuted to one of life imprisonment,\u201d he points out,\u00a0 and contends that a prisoner serving a life sentence cannot now be hanged. This is what has hitherto happened.\n\nNevertheless, people\u2019s opposition to death penalty is in a way strange, as they in most cases either support elimination not only their sworn enemies but sometimes those who hold differing opinions,\u00a0 or keep silent when such eliminations occur. And especially in politics, almost all political parties in Sri Lanka have been involved in extrajudicial killings or ironically defended such crimes while opposing official death sentences.\n\nOver 10,000 young men and women, mostly innocent people,\u00a0 were killed by the armed forces and the police during the first insurrection of the JVP in 1971, while the JVP on its part also took away the right of life of\u00a0 thousands of those seen by them as government informants. The number of extra judicial killings in the south by the armed forces and\u00a0 vigilante groups - PRRA and Green Tigers - during the second insurrection of the JVP was placed at more than 60.000. One wonders if the\u00a0 JVP could\u00a0 have had such a large number of armed fighters."
  },
