    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "During the 1965 Indo-Pakistan War, the MiG-21 did not play a major role as the IAF had only 12-16 of these at the time, and they were not fully operational. But it was the\u00a0 was the IAF\u2019s mainstay during the 1971 war with Pakistan. The IAF seems to have fallen in love with the aircraft, putting it into uses it wasn\u2019t originally designed for. It\u00a0 showed its abilities as a fighter bomber in the 1999 Kargil conflict. By then, several women pilots too, were flying the MiG-21, and this versatile interceptor made its final combat appearance in the subcontinent when\u00a0 a MiG-21 engaged a Pakistani F-16 on February 27, 2019\u00a0 over Jammu and Kashmir,\u00a0 and was shot down.\n\nIn the 2019 Kargil conflict, the IAF used its MiG-21s as night bombers carrying laser bombs for precision bombing, and the then IAF commander said these operations were crucial in defeating the enemy.\n\nMiG-21s were used in large numbers by Egyptian and Syrian air forces in their wars with Israel. Several Arab aces flying the MiG-21 are credited with downing Israeli Mirage and Phantom fighter jets, but information on these combats is hard to obtain. Large Syrian air losses in the latter stages of the 1973 Yom Kippur war were due to data supplied by American AWACs aircraft, which gave the Israelis a tremendous advantage.\n\nMore than\u00a0 11,000 MiG-21 variants were built worldwide. Additionally, India and Czechoslovakia built 851 more under license. China made thousands of license-built reverse-engineered variants such as Chengdu J-7 and these were sold to roughly 60 countries. It was ideal for third world air forces, and cheaper to buy and maintain than any Western interceptor. It could fly from rough, short air fields while its American counterparts required the finest concrete. Elegantly simple, it was designed to get off the ground fast, approach the target quickly and destroy it. The skies over North Vietnam amply proved its abilities."
