    "chunk_size_bytes": 1674,
    "chunk_text": "Now rewind history to the early 20th century, when World War I broke out. There was no Saudi Arabia then. Ottoman Sultan Mehmed V declared jihad, or holy war, calling on the world\u2019s Muslims to join his empire\u2019s fight against the Allies, led by Britain. Instead of responding to the call, many Arabs rebelled. Wahhabi imams such as Sulaiman ibn Sahman from the Najd province in present\u2011day Saudi Arabia issued fatwas to justify the rebellion in religious terms. Ibn Sahman\u2019s fatwa declared the Muslim Ottomans kafirs (infidels) and hailed the British as Ahl al\u2011Kitab (People of the Scriptures), urging Muslims to join the Britain\u2011led Allies.\n\nFrom the post\u2013World War I division of the Middle East to August 7, 2026, much blood has flowed under the bridge. In the interim, Saudi Arabia\u2019s King Abdulaziz Ibn Saud met President Roosevelt in 1945 to establish a defence partnership; the secular Turkish Republic joined the North Atlantic Treaty Organisation in 1952; and Pakistan threw its weight behind the Western bloc during the Cold War, later siding with a rising China, and now seeks to balance between China and Donald Trump\u2019s United States.\n\nAn attempt at unity after a century of division is indeed a welcome move.\n\nSome have called the August 7 deal a \u2018Muslim NATO\u2019 because it contains an Article 5\u2011like clause warning any aggressor that an attack on one will be regarded as an attack on all. This raises serious security concerns with global implications, since all three nations face unresolved disputes \u2018 Pakistan with India and Afghanistan; Saudi Arabia with Iran, the Houthis in Yemen, and other regional rivals; and T\u00fcrkiye with Greece."
  },
