    "chunk_text": "\u201cThis isn\u2019t traditional BPO, nameless, faceless person. This is a core team member, that you know by name, you see every day, and is firmly embedded,\u201d Wallaert noted. Illustrating this deep integration, Wallaert shared that US founders have previously travelled to Sri Lanka specifically to attend their local team members\u2019 weddings, while other divers have flown to the US to participate in client-wide corporate retreats.\n\n\u201cWe see this sort of tenacity coupled with calmness, I think they bring a certain sort of maturity to the job that really stands out,\u201d he explained. Contrasting this with the highly competitive corporate dynamics found in other regional markets, he added, \u201cIt\u2019s that cooperative, \u2018go farther together\u2019 kind of attitude. It\u2019s one of the things I love about working here, is that desire to do things with people rather than against people... it\u2019s not so much the skills as the culture that I think makes Sri Lanka distinct.\u201d\n\nThis cultural philosophy extends internally to how Oceans manages its feedback and corporate environment. Moving away from rigid corporate hierarchies, the firm prioritises calm and constructive communication. Highlighting this shift, Wallaert recalled an exit interview with a departing employee who noted, \u201cThe greatest thing about working at Oceans was that nobody was yelling at me.\u201d\n\nThe market demand for this globally integrated employment model has been massive, with Oceans receiving approximately 20,000 applications annually in Sri Lanka.\n\n\u201cEvery year we want to double in size, so hopefully next year we\u2019ll go from 1,000 to 2,000, 2,000 to 4,000,\u201d Wallaert stated."
