    "chunk_text": "Audit exposes decades of environmental compliance gaps, millions sunk in ghost projects at BOI\n\nData from CEA\u2019s 2023 report confirm that only 7 of BOI\u2019s 12 export processing zones held CEA-recognised Environmental Protection Licenses, backing Auditor General findings of a 30-year regulatory bypass in flagship hubs including Katunayake, Biyagama, and Koggala\n\nInfrastructure failures span key industrial parks, from untreated wastewater and expired licenses to delayed sludge pit construction in zones bordering the Kelani River, source of roughly 80 per cent of Colombo\u2019s drinking water\n\nMillions of rupees in public funds face write-off after being sunk into capital projects where the BOI held no land and no acquisition process was underway\n\nThe Board of Investment (BOI) is facing regulatory pressure following an Auditor General\u2019s report that has laid bare environmental compliance failures and capital expenditure lapses across Sri Lanka\u2019s export processing zones. The audit, contained in the BOI\u2019s 2024 Annual Report released last month, found that major industrial hubs have operated for over three decades without the mandatory concurrence of the Central Environmental Authority (CEA).\n\nA regulatory gap has persisted since the early 1990s, with the apex investment agency bypassing the national environmental regulator to issue its own compliance certificates. According to official audit responses contained in the report, \u201cSince 1992, the practice for issuing Environmental Protection Licenses (EPLs) to industries located within the designated zones has been, that the Board of Investment (BOI) issues EPLs directly, without obtaining concurrence from the Central Environmental Authority (CEA).\u201d"
