    "md5": "2623de1909d14317246942c086e1f050",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1575,
    "chunk_text": "Which to my mind at least raises the Saturday morning question: is Rosamund Pike the new Princess Di? (On Monday, we may grow far more serious about attendant matters!)\n\nWell, not quite. But maybe there is a more serious point behind the perhaps flippant comparison.\n\nBut if her celebrity status can persuade governments, donors, and the comfortably oblivious to pay closer attention to the painstaking business of making the once war-torn soil of Sri Lanka safe again, then her presence is rather more useful than another soon-forgotten photo op and paparazzi feeding frenzy.\n\nAnd behind the flashing lights and hype at having a cinematic artiste of her ilk here, there is quite a story to tell\u2026\n\nSri Lanka has now recovered more than one million anti-personnel mines. MAG says its teams alone have made nearly 103 square kilometres safe since beginning mine-clearing work here in 2002.\n\nThe national-level figures show some 231 square kilometres cleared overall, and over 1,007,660 anti-personnel mines recovered. Mine casualties, once limping along painfully at some 250 annually, have fallen to just three in 2024. And mine-clearing action has also enabled the return of roughly 918,000 displaced people, which figure is often conventionally rounded up in current reporting to roughly a million.\n\nThese are not simply public-relations numbers, although in a sense they can serve as some sort of positive propaganda. In fact, they are the physical statistics of peace \u2013 where once the terrific tolls of a war-riven nation dominated the news and public concern indices."
  },
