    "md5": "e07437ad2253e9ada66c6fe42afd2fea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1583,
    "chunk_text": "The numbers and the circumstantial evidence are appalling. But here truth requires restraint. The grim facts of Chemmani do not yet prove that 515 people were murdered by the Sri Lankan security forces. It does not establish that all the dead were Tamil civilians, although the excavation has revived the longstanding allegation that hundreds of Tamils who disappeared after the military recaptured Jaffna in 1995 were killed and buried there. It does not yet identify the dead. And it does not yet establish criminal responsibility.\n\nIn 1998, a soldier testified that between 300 and 400 people had been buried there. The original 1999 excavation recovered 15 skeletons. Now, nearly three decades later, the soil is producing human remains on a scale that has surpassed the original allegation.\n\nA country that is serious about reclaiming its ethos on the world stage after a brutal war in which many atrocities were allegedly committed by all combatants cannot respond to that merely by changing the subject to a glamorous actress reincarnating the spirit of a ministering angel princess. To its credit, the present Government has not done so. The excavation is proceeding at present under State and judicial supervision.\n\nThe NPP Government has allocated funds for the latest phase of the investigation and the Justice Ministry has said international forensic assistance will be sought for DNA identification. The administration has also pumped additional money into the Office on Missing Persons (OMP) and says it intends to establish a Truth and Reconciliation Commission (TRC)."
