    "md5": "9f8db5a821a24af4cd2e6b39c0432079",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1450,
    "chunk_text": "But better not make the beautiful but asymmetrical mistake of assuming that the two are equal and opposite elements. Sri Lanka is now confronting two very different legacies of the war at the same time, the physical legacy which it has handled remarkably well, and the evidentiary and moral legacy, where its track record remains tarnished still, and image and institution building an unfinished task\u2026\n\nAnd if Pike and her Diana-like ilk can help persuade the world to put some money behind the task of demining while encouraging Sri Lanka to summon the courage to confront its past demons, then perhaps the Princess Di comparison is not entirely flippant after all.\n\nDiana was politically potent \u2013 even explosive \u2013 because she bravely entered a minefield, and for the first time made the landmine a powerful element in an international humanitarian campaign. Pike comes to Sri Lanka at a time when its last mines are being defused. And she serves by bringing our country\u2019s global audience back for a last symbolic as well as substantive act. So \u2018PR exercise\u2019 and \u2018humanitarian act\u2019 are not mutually exclusive, thankfully.\n\nFor now, perhaps the true heroes are the de-miners themselves. And ultimately, the real test is whether Sri Lanka has the courage of its convictions to clear both kinds of minefields.\n\n(The writer is the Editor-at-large of LMD and is a senior journalist with a Post-graduate Diploma in Politics and Governance)"
