    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka strengthens investor links with Australia\n\nThe Securities and Exchange Commission of Sri Lanka (SEC), Colombo Stock Exchange (CSE) and stockbroking industry are conducting a series of Invest Sri Lanka investor forums in Australia, with multiple forums to be held in Melbourne and Sydney.\n\nThe forums will be held in Sydney and Melbourne from August 17, 2026 to August 20, 2026.\n\nInvest Sri Lanka Investor Forum \u2013 Australia 2026 follows the Invest Sri Lanka investor forums held in Dubai and Riyadh earlier in January.\n\nCapitalising on the growth and stability achieved in the Sri Lankan capital market in 2025, the Invest Sri Lanka investor forums of 2026 are set to leverage on Sri Lanka\u2019s status as an attractive frontier market and will seek to attract institutional investors and the Sri Lankan diaspora.\n\nThe SEC and CSE will be returning to Australia after nearly nine years, following the last Invest Sri Lanka Investor Forum Australia conducted in 2017.\n\nThe 2017 roadshow played an important role in strengthening investor awareness of Sri Lanka among the investors in Australia, with the buy turnover rising over 700 percent from Rs.72.9 million to Rs.603.8 million within the six-month window, following the forums.\n\nAs of end-2025, the buy turnover has risen to Rs.1.85 billion, with Australia emerging as one of the top 10 sources of foreign investor inflows into the Sri Lankan capital market and as one of the top 10 sources of remittance inflows from the growing Sri Lankan diaspora in Australia.\n\nWith the strong rise in Sri Lanka\u2019s All-Share-Price-Index (ASPI) over the past 10 years, a rising number of Sri Lankan diaspora investors have taken an interest in the Sri Lankan stock market.\n\nThe forums will be conducted amidst a remarkable six-year growth in the capital market, which saw the ASPI rise from 4,846 points in May 2020 to 22,310.80 points by end-May 2026. This represents a growth of 360 percent and a compound annual growth rate of approximately 28.98 percent."
