    "md5": "ac776269167c962746cd38a706d5fad5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1034,
    "chunk_text": "NPP at a Constitutional Crossroads: Pragmatism, Provincial Democracy and the Price of Public Trust\n\nThe National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government of Sri Lanka is currently at a pivotal moment, challenged to fulfill its mandate for transparency, accountability, and governance that prioritises the public interest. This imperative is especially pressing in light of the upcoming elections for Provincial Councils and the implications of the Thirteenth Amendment as the NPP navigates its relationship with India.\n\nThe establishment of a Select Committee to address the electoral framework is an important step, especially given that Sri Lanka has not held full Provincial Council elections for over a decade. While India\u2019s call for prompt elections adds a diplomatic angle, it is essential to frame these elections as a matter of Sri Lanka\u2019s own constitutional integrity. The Thirteenth Amendment requires elected Provincial Councils, and discussions about reform should focus on democratic principles rather than prolonged delays."
