    "language": "en",
    "md5": "87dd430ceb0273fd35d67d13b9dd480b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "It nationalised the tea estates, took over distribution of petroleum products and even closed down the Israeli embassy in the country in 1971. This despite pro-Israel groups in the United Kingdom threatening to boycott Ceylon tea if Lanka persisted with the move.\n\nAt that time,\u00a0 the export of tea was Lanka\u2019s biggest exchange earner. But the then government proceeded with the closure of the Embassy regardless of threats.\n\nThe then government instructed the team led by Anura Tennakoon to boycott the fixture due to its foreign policy of supporting the Palestinian cause and in opposition to Israel\u2019s continued violation of human rights in Palestine. Because Lanka refused to take the field, the match was recorded as a walkover and Israel was awarded points.\n\nDespite losing those points, Sri Lanka topped their group on net run rate and ultimately won the ICC Trophy tournament!\n\nUnfortunately today, our government does not seem to be in a position to even explain to these modern-day delusional Don Quixotes that these old British puritanical beliefs, once imposed on our people, have now become deeply ingrained into our religio-cultural milieu. These beliefs cannot be changed overnight with a stroke of a pen. It will take time for large numbers of people to accept these \u2018new\u2019 beliefs.\n\nIn the event the government hastily adopts the required legislation to please our new neo-colonial masters, we will see a tidal wave of opposition led by religious leaders. Soon,\u00a0 opportunistic politicians\u00a0 will be joining hands with religious leaders calling the decriminalisng of same-sex relations as an attack on our culture. They may even demand a referendum be held on the matter.\n\nWhat is even stranger perhaps is that even in the EU countries, attacks on the LGBTIQ community are growing. Data and human rights reports indicate that harassment, hate speech, and physical attacks targeting the LGBTQ+ community are growing across parts of Europe."
  },
