    "chunk_size_bytes": 1624,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cIf there is doubt about the integrity of any part of that process, the credibility of the entire prosecution case may ultimately be affected,\u201d he said, adding that the risk became even more serious when the punishment under consideration was death.\n\nKuruppu also cautioned that human errors could occur within the judicial process itself.\n\nHe said the Supreme Court subsequently set aside those Court of Appeal judgments and sent the cases back for reconsideration or retrial.\n\nHe further highlighted the issue of economic inequality in access to justice.\n\nHe said an accused person with financial resources might be able to retain an experienced lawyer and obtain expert assistance, while an extremely poor accused might lack the resources required to obtain scientific evidence, call expert witnesses or properly challenge weaknesses in an investigation.\n\n\u201cIf a person\u2019s life is at stake, economic circumstances should never determine the quality of the legal defence available to that person,\u201d he said.\n\nHe therefore called on the State to ensure effective legal representation for every accused person, regardless of their financial circumstances, before the death penalty is implemented.\n\nHe also advocated greater reliance on DNA, fingerprints, digital evidence, CCTV footage and other forms of scientific and technological evidence rather than depending solely on eyewitness testimony, statements or confessions.\n\nHe said Sri Lanka should invest further in forensic science and strengthen the capacity of investigators and forensic institutions to properly collect, analyse and present scientific evidence."
