    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "He also advocated greater reliance on DNA, fingerprints, digital evidence, CCTV footage and other forms of scientific and technological evidence rather than depending solely on eyewitness testimony, statements or confessions.\n\nHe said Sri Lanka should invest further in forensic science and strengthen the capacity of investigators and forensic institutions to properly collect, analyse and present scientific evidence.\n\nHe also called for effective legal representation for indigent accused persons, independent mechanisms to investigate serious complaints concerning criminal investigations, stronger mechanisms to review potentially wrongful convictions and the highest level of appellate and judicial safeguards in death penalty cases.\n\nKuruppu stressed that his position did not amount to opposition to severe punishment for serious crimes.\n\n\u201cVictims deserve justice. Their families deserve justice. Criminal offenders must be held accountable under the law,\u201d he said, while emphasising that this must be achieved through a fair investigation and reliable judicial process.\n\nHe noted that Sri Lanka had maintained a long-standing moratorium on executions, although the death penalty remained part of the country\u2019s legal framework.\n\nThe central question, he said, should therefore not simply be whether executions should resume, but whether the criminal justice system was sufficiently reliable to prevent an innocent person from being convicted because of investigative failures, fabricated evidence, inadequate legal representation or judicial error.\n\n\u201cA judicial conviction can be overturned. A prison sentence can be corrected. A person can be released and compensated, but a human life cannot be restored once it has been taken,\u201d Kuruppu said."
