    "chunk_size_bytes": 1527,
    "chunk_text": "Legal battles loom against 22nd amendment\n\nColombo, August 15 (Daily Mirror) - Legal battles against the proposed 22nd Amendment are expected to get off the ground next week as the groups in the legal fraternity are expected to file petitions against the legislation as the government prepares to table the legislation in Parliament on August 18. A group of President\u2019s Counsel and the joint opposition are expected to file petitions against the legislation next week.\n\nConvener of the Joint Opposition Professor G. L. Peiris has already revealed that they will file petitions against the legislation and would ask the Supreme Court to rule that it has to be approved by the people at a referendum.\n\nA group of leading President\u2019s Counsel said they will also file a petition against the amendment next week.\n\nPresident Counsel Tisath Wijegunawardene who is a member of a group of President\u2019s Counsel who had opposed the legislation said his group will also file a petition next week. \u201cWe will also call the Supreme Court to declare that the legislation should approved by the people at a referendum. He said they will cite the verdict given by a three member bench comprising Supreme Court judges Buwaneka Aluwihare, Murdu N. B. Fernando and Janak de Silva in 2022 which said any Constitutional amendment to the retirement age or the period of office impacting on incumbent judges, whether directly or indirectly, will impinge on the independence of the judiciary and violative of Article 3 which requires a Referendum."
