    "chunk_id": "2026-08-17-dailymirrorlk-afad4530-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "46abc7708cf4405b6a8cf5f498db3f86",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1811,
    "chunk_text": "DFCC Bank 2Q earnings fall as costs offset stronger fee income\n\nDFCC Bank profit fell 17 percent year-on-year to Rs. 2.33 billion in the second quarter of 2026, as a sharp increase in operating expenses outweighed stronger fee income and lower impairment charges.\n\nGroup profit before tax declined 21 percent to Rs. 3.23 billion from Rs. 4.07 billion a year earlier, while earnings per share fell to Rs. 5.12 from Rs. 6.40.\n\nThe earnings decline came despite an improvement in net operating income, highlighting the impact of a rapidly expanding cost base during the quarter.\n\nGroup total operating income rose just 2 percent to Rs. 11.57 billion, while impairment charges fell 31 percent to Rs. 1.47 billion. That helped net operating income increase 10 percent to Rs. 10.10 billion. Operating expenses, however, surged 42 percent to Rs. 5.74 billion from Rs. 4.05 billion a year earlier. Personnel expenses increased 84 percent to Rs. 2.60 billion, while other expenses rose 18 percent to Rs. 2.67 billion. As a result, operating profit before taxes on financial services fell 15 percent to Rs. 4.36 billion.\n\nAt the Bank level, which accounts for the bulk of Group operations, second-quarter profit after tax fell 20 percent to Rs. 2.19 billion, while profit before tax declined 23 percent to Rs. 3.04 billion.\n\nNet interest income at the Bank rose just 1 percent to Rs. 7.81 billion as a 17 percent increase in interest expenses largely absorbed an 11 percent rise in interest income. Net fee and commission income provided stronger support, increasing 25 percent to Rs. 2.27 billion.\n\nDFCC attributed the higher cost base partly to annual salary revisions and performance-based incentive payments, alongside continued investment in technology and digital infrastructure, marketing and business development."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-17-dailymirrorlk-afad4530",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-afad4530",
    "date_str": "2026-08-17",
    "description": "DFCC Bank 2Q earnings fall as costs offset stronger fee income",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/DFCC-Bank-2Q-earnings-fall-as-costs-offset-stronger-fee-income/273-348218",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786910522.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-17-dailymirrorlk-afad4530-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "69d6b56cd6fc52f3948bb990b2fca2cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1781,
    "chunk_text": "DFCC attributed the higher cost base partly to annual salary revisions and performance-based incentive payments, alongside continued investment in technology and digital infrastructure, marketing and business development.\n\nFor the six months ended June, Group profit after tax from continuing operations fell 28 percent to Rs. 4.14 billion from Rs. 5.75 billion, while Group profit before tax declined 29 percent to Rs. 5.80 billion from Rs. 8.17 billion. Profit attributable to equity holders fell 30 percent to Rs. 4.05 billion.\n\nAt the Bank level, first-half profit after tax from core operations declined 30 percent to Rs. 3.90 billion from Rs. 5.56 billion, while profit before tax fell 31 percent to Rs. 5.48 billion.\n\nChief Executive Officer Thimal Perera acknowledged the weaker earnings, saying the performance reflected decisions taken to strengthen the lender\u2019s risk buffers.\n\n\u201cReported profitability was lower than in the corresponding period,\u201d Perera said, adding that the bank had \u201cstrengthened impairment provisioning through model refinements and management overlays\u201d while maintaining a selective approach to lending.\n\nFirst-half impairment charges increased 33 percent to Rs. 4.63 billion from Rs. 3.48 billion. DFCC said it strengthened provisioning in response to global and domestic economic risks, including additional management overlays for exposures to higher-risk sectors and customer segments and provisions for specific large-group exposures.\n\nThe higher provisioning came alongside stronger underlying income generation. At Group level, net interest income increased 6 percent to Rs. 16.17 billion, while net fee and commission income rose 29 percent to Rs. 4.19 billion. Group total operating income increased 6 percent to Rs. 23.66 billion."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-17-dailymirrorlk-afad4530",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-afad4530",
    "date_str": "2026-08-17",
    "description": "DFCC Bank 2Q earnings fall as costs offset stronger fee income",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/DFCC-Bank-2Q-earnings-fall-as-costs-offset-stronger-fee-income/273-348218",
    "lang": "en",
