    "chunk_size_bytes": 1701,
    "chunk_text": "Strict conditions placed on pre-May LC exemptions to curb vehicle trade loopholes\n\nSurcharge exemption voided if LC details altered or shipments delayed past mid-November\n\nFreight operators and green mobility transition face unexpected cost burdens\n\nImporters relying on early banking documents to bypass the government\u2019s newly extended 50 percent vehicle import duty surcharge face a precarious path forward, with stringent regulatory conditions threatening to disqualify numerous shipments.\n\nThe Ministry of Finance, Planning and Economic Development has outlined strict criteria for vehicles imported under Letters of Credit (LCs) established on or before May 15, 2026, aiming to close trade loopholes.\n\nThe departmental order, issued under Section 10A of the Customs Ordinance, extends the surcharge on the applicable Customs Import Duty on a \u201cboth General and Preferential basis, with effect from August 15, 2026 to December 31, 2026\u201d. Signed by President Anura Kumara Dissanayake in his capacity as the Minister of Finance, Planning and Economic Development, the document stipulates that while the pre-May LC exemption remains in place, it will be immediately revoked if import documentation is modified.\n\nThe order explicitly states the surcharge applies \u201cif any one or several of the following have been amended i.e. number of vehicles, vehicle identification number, description of the vehicle, technical specifications, date of expiry of the Letter of Credit/ Letters of Credit\u201d. Furthermore, a strict shipping deadline has been enforced; consignments will automatically incur the levy if the \u201cshipped on board date of Bill of Lading/Airway Bill falls after November 15, 2026\u201d."
  },
