    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1d69d0240a091629d2ea0d2af9a7e6fa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "Stop worshipping growth, start measuring poverty, economists tell government\n\nPoverty rate more than doubled to nearly 30 percent during the crisis, but the state has published no official measure since\n\nEconomists say policy is captured by a small circle of vested interests - \u201c100 families\u201d- blocking competition and enterprise\n\nHigh real interest rates quietly transferring wealth from wage earners to capital holders, panel warns\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s obsession with headline GDP growth is masking a deepening poverty crisis that the state has failed to even measure, leading economists warned, calling for an urgent shift towards a national development plan that confronts the country\u2019s entrenched vested interests.\n\nSpeaking at a panel discussion hosted by the Sri Lanka\u2013Korea Business Council titled \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s Future \u2013 Forecast, Scenarios and Challenges,\u201d Verit\u00e9 Research Executive Director Dr. Nishan de Mel said growth that fails to improve ordinary lives will eventually destabilise the country, including for those who benefit from it.\n\n\u201cWe mustn\u2019t confuse means and ends. The point of growth is for us to live better lives, and for the flourishing of humans and the planet. Growth doesn\u2019t become the master, it must become the servant. And as economists, we often forget that. We start worshipping growth, but not the outcomes on human lives,\u201d de Mel said.\n\nThe blind spot, he argued, is statistical as much as political. Sri Lanka\u2019s poverty rate more than doubled from 14.3 percent to nearly 30 percent during the crisis\u2014a steeper deterioration than in other debt-distressed nations\u2014yet the state has not published an official poverty measure since. \u201cWhat you fail to measure, you fail to focus on,\u201d de Mel said, noting that roughly 92 percent of formal private sector employees earn less than Rs. 100,000 a month."
  },
