    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5f0450d768fb566a8c572779411fff12",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1504,
    "chunk_text": "Kethesh Loganathan and the Lost Generation\n\nMany from Kethesh\u2019s generation dreamt of social change and willingly sacrificed their future, when others sought greener pastures in distant lands. That generation of Tamil activists, if not killed in battle, or assassinated, were broken by the trauma of a violent political culture\n\nTwenty years is a long time. I often think of Kethesh Loganathan over these years, even though I had actively known him for just four years. My father had been in touch with Kethesh for decades, and his introduction began a friendship and collaboration with Kethesh which suddenly ended with his assassination on 12 August 2006.\n\nThe Tamil community and the country as a whole have lost tremendous activists whose lives were cut short by a violent political culture. Their passion for social change could have reshaped the future of the country, if they had lived to make fuller contributions in the political and economic realms. I write about Kethesh here, because I feel it is important that the younger generations know about such courageous figures, their commitment to dissent and their tragic sacrifices.\n\nKethesh came from a well-known Tamil family, and was the son of the first Sri Lankan General Manager of the Bank of Ceylon. He was educated in the United States, and some of best social science institutions in Europe. Despite his elite upbringing, he was drawn by the spirit of many Tamil youth of his generation, and he joined one of the Tamil militant groups."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-17-dailymirrorlk-a9f62d24",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-a9f62d24",
    "date_str": "2026-08-17",
    "description": "Kethesh Loganathan and the Lost Generation",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Kethesh-Loganathan-and-the-Lost-Generation/172-348228",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786914968.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-17-dailymirrorlk-a9f62d24-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "47fb4d9705268c8dbcd94d5a670e9776",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1106,
    "chunk_text": "Kethesh came from a well-known Tamil family, and was the son of the first Sri Lankan General Manager of the Bank of Ceylon. He was educated in the United States, and some of best social science institutions in Europe. Despite his elite upbringing, he was drawn by the spirit of many Tamil youth of his generation, and he joined one of the Tamil militant groups.\n\nAs disagreements rose among the EPRLF leadership, Kethesh left the EPRLF in the mid-1990s and sought a career first in journalism, and then as a political analyst on the conflict. It is during this time that he wrote an important book on the search for a political solution in Sri Lanka titled, \u2018Lost Opportunities\u2019. He was well positioned to write that book, because he was a participant during one of the early negotiations towards a political solution in 1985, facilitated by India between the Government of Sri Lanka and a number of Tamil militant groups and political parties in Thimpu. Even though the negotiations failed, Kethesh was central to formulating the Tamil nationalist position now famously known as the Thimpu Principles."
  },
