    "md5": "dc59bb8497760facee0606abf30b9078",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1342,
    "chunk_text": "While the Colombo establishment, including most NGOs were aligned with the Norwegian approach, Kethesh was one of the few progressive voices in Colombo to challenge the internationalised peace process backed by the US, EU and Japan. This was a context in which Kethesh also had to worry about his safety, and indeed many of us feared for his life, as most Tamil critics had rarely been spared by the LTTE.\n\nIn those years, preceding his assassination, I was constantly in touch with him. A number of us living overseas sought to use the safety and space abroad, to write and speak critically about the worrying developments and unfolding disaster. In the three years before his untimely death, he became one of my mentors. Kethesh as I knew him was a loner, and once he arrived at a position it was hard to get him to shift. But with the few of us he chose to engage, he would listen to the disagreements carefully, and only when he was fully convinced, would he shift his position.\n\nAfter his demise, and as the war escalated, I used to think to myself, how Kethesh would have responded to a question or a situation. And now, over seventeen years after the war, I often wonder how much Kethesh could have contributed to the debates and developments during our troubled post-war journey. The loss most of all has been for the Tamil community."
  },
