    "md5": "965408450094f5563a85d9cbe98f72e2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1704,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka does very little of this. Parliament doesn\u2019t take public testimony on the budget or on audit reports. There\u2019s no standing mechanism for civil society or ordinary citizens to feed into how spending decisions get made. The National Audit Office comes closest to offering an entry point, rated as providing \u201cadequate\u201d oversight, but it isn\u2019t really built as a public participation channel either.\n\nOversight came in at 56, mostly on the strength of that same audit office. The Enacted Budget, the final version passed into law, scored 89 out of 100 for how thoroughly it\u2019s published. And for the first time, Sri Lanka put out a Citizens Budget this year: a simplified version of the national budget aimed at the general public.\n\nNone of that closes the participation gap. If anything, it makes the gap more obvious. The government has gotten noticeably better at putting information out, without getting any better at inviting a response to it.\n\nA few gaps explain a good chunk of the low score. Sri Lanka doesn\u2019t publish a Pre-Budget Statement, the document meant to lay out the government\u2019s fiscal plans before the budget itself is drafted, so citizens have something to react to early. It hasn\u2019t produced a Mid-Year Review, which would show whether spending is tracking to plan partway through the fiscal year. And the In-Year Reports it does publish tend to come out late, which cuts into how useful they are for anyone trying to follow spending as it happens.\n\nThe survey\u2019s authors also note that Sri Lanka has no independent fiscal institution: the kind of body that, in other countries, gives outside analysis of budget decisions without being part of government itself."
  },
