    "md5": "58415372d589b447ab38733cb2b1aa24",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1969,
    "chunk_text": "The survey\u2019s authors also note that Sri Lanka has no independent fiscal institution: the kind of body that, in other countries, gives outside analysis of budget decisions without being part of government itself.\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake, who also holds the finance portfolio, has already started a round of pre-budget review meetings with individual ministries, beginning with the Ministry of Justice on August 5, after a broader session in July where he told officials that delays on policy decisions would not be tolerated in the lead-up to the budget. Government statements have described this as part of \u201ca more consultative approach to public expenditure planning.\u201d\n\nSo far, the consultation reported has been between the President and ministry officials, not the public. There\u2019s no indication yet of a published Pre-Budget Statement open for comment, public hearings on spending priorities, or a way for citizens or civil society groups to weigh in before the draft goes to Cabinet in September. Whether that changes before November 12 is, in effect, the next real test of the participation gap the Open Budget Survey documented.\n\nOn transparency specifically, Sri Lanka still sits below the regional average of 49, trailing Nepal (52), Pakistan (45), and India (44), though it stays ahead of Bangladesh (37). Participation figures for regional peers weren\u2019t broken out the same way in the material reviewed for this piece, but a score of 9 out of 100 is low by almost any comparison.\n\nThe International Budget Partnership\u2019s recommendations for Sri Lanka are fairly specific: publish the Pre-Budget Statement and In-Year Reports online and on schedule, start producing Mid-Year Reviews, and let parliament hear public testimony on both budget proposals and audit findings. None of that requires new legislation or a change to the country\u2019s fiscal framework. It requires the government to open a door that, so far, it has mostly kept shut."
