    "md5": "b0112aec503deb69528ff43b0394228d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "Logistics carries Aitken Spence as tourism sinks deeper into loss\n\nAitken Spence PLC\u2019s logistics and strategic investment businesses carried the conglomerate through a difficult quarter for tourism, helping the attributable profit nearly triple even as the group revenue slipped.\n\nThe profit attributable to the shareholders surged 172 percent year-on-year to Rs.1.45 billion in the three months ended June 30, 2026, from Rs.533.7 million, according to the group\u2019s interim financial statements.\n\nOverall group profit, including the losses attributable to the minority shareholders, increased 158 percent to Rs.1.04 billion. The difference reflected a Rs.407.1 million loss attributable to the non-controlling interests, which widened from Rs.128.4 million a year earlier.\n\nThe stronger bottom line came despite the group revenue declining 1.2 percent to Rs.18.96 billion, from Rs.19.19 billion. Net revenue eased to Rs.18.53 billion, from Rs.18.71 billion.\n\nThe earnings were lifted by a sharp increase in other operating income to Rs.1.98 billion, from Rs.223.8 million. While the filing did not provide a breakdown of the increase, the group\u2019s cash-flow disclosures showed a net foreign-exchange gain of Rs.784.9 million, compared with Rs.167.6 million a year earlier.\n\nThe operating profit rose 33 percent to Rs.2.05 billion, even as the employee costs increased 14 percent to Rs.5.10 billion and the indirect operating expenses climbed 8.8 percent to Rs.4.27 billion.\n\nA higher contribution from the equity-accounted investees also supported the earnings. The group\u2019s share of profit from these businesses increased 74 percent to Rs.941.9 million, from Rs.540.3 million.\n\nThe gains outweighed a 16 percent increase in finance expenses to Rs.1.75 billion. The net finance costs rose to Rs.1.25 billion, from Rs.1.08 billion.\n\nProfit before tax climbed 74 percent to Rs.1.74 billion, while the income-tax charge rose at a slower 17 percent to Rs.699.7 million."
