    "language": "en",
    "md5": "94d19919d0443d1279265769e62fade9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1784,
    "chunk_text": "Discussions also covered the rapid adoption of modern technology and advanced equipment by the defence sector, as well as increased allocations for overseas training to improve the professional skills and knowledge of defence personnel.\n\nDeputy Minister of Defence Major General Aruna Jayasekara, Secretary to the President Dr. Nandika Sanath Kumanayake, Chief of Presidential Staff Prabath Chandrakeerthi, Defence Ministry Secretary Air Vice Marshal Sampath Thuyacontha and Treasury Secretary Dr. Harshana Sooriyapperuma were among those present, along with the Commanders of the three Armed Forces and other officials.\n\nAmong the major allocations in the gazetted 2026 Appropriation Bill, the Defence Ministry was allocated Rs. 455 billion, comprising Rs. 395 billion in recurrent expenditure and Rs.60 billion in capital expenditure. This compared with Rs. 634.8 billion for the Finance, Planning and Economic Development Ministry, Rs. 555 billion for the Health and Mass Media Ministry, and Rs. 301 billion for the Education, Higher Education and Vocational Education Ministry.\n\nVerit\u00e9 Research, in its \u2018State of the Budget 2026\u2019, flagged the trajectory of spending on diets and uniforms across the defence sector. It said that although such expenditure is expected to decline by 12.7% in 2026, total spending on diets and uniforms across the sector is projected to have increased by 299% between 2022 and 2026, compared with cumulative inflation of about 80% over the same period.\n\nVerit\u00e9 Research had previously raised concerns over a threefold increase in this expenditure within the Defence Ministry in 2024. While the Ministry\u2019s overall budget remained stable in 2025, it said the provision for diets and uniforms under the Public Security Ministry increased 17-fold."
  },
