    "chunk_text": "\u2018Between the Lamp and the Pot:\u2019 A referendum remembered, and a warning for today\n\nDespite these cordial appearances, President J. R. Jayawardene\u00a0 deprived former Prime Minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike\u00a0 of her civic rights\n\nIn 1982, democracy was threatened by a referendum. Forty-four years later, the danger may lie at the opposite extreme\n\nThere is much talk about referendums today, and it takes me back 44 years to Sri Lanka\u2019s first, and so far only, national referendum, held in December 1982. President J. R. Jayewardene (JRJ) called it to extend the life of Parliament by another six years. The political circumstances were very different from today: the government wanted the referendum, while the opposition strongly opposed it.\n\nBut the statistics and constitutional arguments tell only part of the story. My own experience at a polling booth in Attidiya gave me a disturbing firsthand glimpse of how elections were conducted then. Over the past thirty years, the powers and independence of the Elections Commissioner have been significantly strengthened, providing greater authority to ensure the integrity and fairness of the electoral process.\n\nTo understand why the referendum was held, one must go back to the 1977 general election, when the UNP won an unprecedented 140 of 168 parliamentary seats. Using this huge majority, the government introduced the 1978 Constitution, established an executive presidency, introduced proportional representation and extended the terms of Parliament and the presidency to six years.\n\nIn October 1982, JR won Sri Lanka\u2019s first direct presidential election with 52% of the vote, while SLFP candidate Hector Kobbekaduwa received 35%. Former Prime Minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike had been deprived of her civic rights and was therefore unable to contest. It was the first time in 30 years that an incumbent party had won a national election in Sri Lanka."
  },
