    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-ac3db9f2-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "118e1c70d60900d30a3bab21b2135b96",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1426,
    "chunk_text": "In October 1982, JR won Sri Lanka\u2019s first direct presidential election with 52% of the vote, while SLFP candidate Hector Kobbekaduwa received 35%. Former Prime Minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike had been deprived of her civic rights and was therefore unable to contest. It was the first time in 30 years that an incumbent party had won a national election in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe solution was to extend Parliament\u2019s term without holding a general election. The government introduced the Fourth Amendment to extend the life of the First Parliament until\u00a0 August 4 1989. The Supreme Court approved the proposal by a 4\u20133 majority, subject to the required parliamentary majority and referendum approval. Parliament passed the bill on\u00a0 November 5 1982 by 142 votes to four.\n\nOn 14 November, JR called the referendum for December 22. Voters were asked whether they approved extending Parliament\u2019s term until 4 August 1989. A \u201cYes\u201d vote was represented by a lamp and a \u201cNo\u201d vote by a pot. Under the Referendum Act of 1981, if less than two-thirds of registered voters participated, at least one-third of all registered voters also had to vote \u201cYes\u201d for the proposal to pass.\n\nThe campaign was bitterly contested. Following the October presidential election, Jayewardene had imposed a state of emergency, claiming that sections of the SLFP were plotting against him. The emergency remained in force during the referendum."
  },
