    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c8777bdedfbad3d000989e8ebdd84a55",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cCyclone Ditwah exposed critical structural gaps in  Sri Lanka\u2019s early warning  architecture\u201d - Prof. Amaratunga -\n\nWe\u2019re also not yet building resilience cumulatively the way we should be. That\u2019s the gap my research tries to address\n\nSri Lanka sits at a particular intersection of vulnerability that makes preparedness not optional, but foundational\n\nProf. Amaratunga conducts joint research with\u00a0Sri Lankan scholars focusing on areas like disaster\u00a0risk management\n\nSri Lanka has witnessed many natural disasters from time to time, ever since the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami to cyclone Ditwah. In each one of these disasters, many lives were lost in addition to damage to property and infrastructure. Researchers have identified gaps in disaster preparedness planning despite Sri Lanka improving considerably in terms of establishing early warning systems and evacuation infrastructure post 2004.\n\nIn this exclusive interview with the Daily Mirror, Dilanthi Amaratunga, professor of Disaster Risks and Climate Resilience at Loughborough University, UK, one of the world\u2019s most cited scholars in built environment disaster resilience, responded to some pertinent questions related to disaster risk reduction and disaster mitigation. In 2019, she won the prestigious Newton Prize alongside Prof. Richard Haigh (University of Huddersfield) and Dr. Harkunti Rahayu (Institut Teknologi Bandung) for their collaborative research project protecting coastal communities from tsunamis and natural hazards in Indonesia. She continues to conduct joint research with Sri Lankan scholars, focusing on disaster risk management, preparedness, mitigation, and governance\n\nQSri Lanka experienced many natural disasters from the tsunami to 2016 floods and cyclone Ditwah. In each one of them, many lives were lost in addition to damage to infrastructure. What are your observations about these natural calamities, loss of lives and damage?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4",
    "date_str": "2026-08-18",
    "description": "\u201cCyclone Ditwah exposed critical structural gaps in  Sri Lanka\u2019s early warning  architecture\u201d - Prof. Amaratunga -",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Cyclone-Ditwah-exposed-critical-structural-gaps-in-Sri-Lankas-early-warning-architecture-Prof-Amaratunga/334-348330",
    "lang": "en",
