    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2528b3f6c7e2a70d8a975ac098cd9078",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1708,
    "chunk_text": "QSri Lanka experienced many natural disasters from the tsunami to 2016 floods and cyclone Ditwah. In each one of them, many lives were lost in addition to damage to infrastructure. What are your observations about these natural calamities, loss of lives and damage?\n\nThe 2004 tsunami killed close to 35,000 people in Sri Lanka and was, understandably, treated as a singular, unprecedented catastrophe, a once-in-a-generation event that justified building an entirely new disaster management architecture, which is exactly what happened with the Disaster Management Act of 2005. But the assumption embedded in that response was that we were preparing for another rare mega-event of that type.\n\nWhat 2016 showed us was different. That disaster, over 100 lives lost, nearly half a million people affected, and the Aranayake landslide alone burying entire villages, wasn\u2019t a freak occurrence. It was described at the time as the worst rainfall in nearly two decades, and it was still recognisably a monsoon-driven flood-and-landslide event, the kind Sri Lanka has always been exposed to. And then in 2017, just a year later, we saw over 200 more deaths from very similar flooding. So the pattern isn\u2019t \u2018rare mega-disaster, then decades of calm\u2019, and it\u2019s recurring, monsoon-linked, compound hazard risk, arriving on a much shorter cycle than our institutions were built to expect.\n\nCyclone Ditwah takes that pattern and escalates it further: over 600 deaths, more than 2 million people affected, and what\u2019s been called the most extensive flooding and landslide damage in two decades, with cascading impacts through health systems, water supply, and livelihoods that are still unresolved months later."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4",
    "date_str": "2026-08-18",
    "description": "\u201cCyclone Ditwah exposed critical structural gaps in  Sri Lanka\u2019s early warning  architecture\u201d - Prof. Amaratunga -",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Cyclone-Ditwah-exposed-critical-structural-gaps-in-Sri-Lankas-early-warning-architecture-Prof-Amaratunga/334-348330",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
