    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "18095aff1b756c02bc8f6f4e00541d98",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1485,
    "chunk_text": "Cyclone Ditwah takes that pattern and escalates it further: over 600 deaths, more than 2 million people affected, and what\u2019s been called the most extensive flooding and landslide damage in two decades, with cascading impacts through health systems, water supply, and livelihoods that are still unresolved months later.\n\nQDid we have opportunities to introduce and implement disaster preparedness and disaster risk mitigation plans in earlier instances?\n\nYes, and this is really the central finding across nearly two decades of my research in this area in Sri Lanka. The opportunities were there, repeatedly, but implementation consistently lagged behind intention. What the research shows is a recurring gap between designing good frameworks and embedding them operationally, with sufficient resourcing, all the way down through local government and frontline response. That gap, more than any lack of technical knowledge, is what I think has cost us the most in repeated events.\n\nTake the tsunami for instance. Our work on post-tsunami knowledge sharing made the case clearly back then, that effective lesson-learning should reduce future risk through well-informed mitigation and preparedness planning. But that depends on having an efficient system for capturing and sharing disaster risk knowledge. We had the raw material for learning. What we lacked, and in many ways still lack, was the systematic infrastructure to convert that knowledge into standing institutional practice."
  },
