    "description": "\u201cCyclone Ditwah exposed critical structural gaps in  Sri Lanka\u2019s early warning  architecture\u201d - Prof. Amaratunga -",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Cyclone-Ditwah-exposed-critical-structural-gaps-in-Sri-Lankas-early-warning-architecture-Prof-Amaratunga/334-348330",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787033553.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c5c8118d03cd8e58113293421537e75a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1655,
    "chunk_text": "Take the tsunami for instance. Our work on post-tsunami knowledge sharing made the case clearly back then, that effective lesson-learning should reduce future risk through well-informed mitigation and preparedness planning. But that depends on having an efficient system for capturing and sharing disaster risk knowledge. We had the raw material for learning. What we lacked, and in many ways still lack, was the systematic infrastructure to convert that knowledge into standing institutional practice.\n\nThe same pattern shows up in early warning. Our research identifying gaps in tsunami early-warning and evacuation procedures, and separately mapping the \u2018upstream-downstream interface\u2019 of Sri Lanka\u2019s tsunami warning system, both point to the same underlying issue: we capture the upstream detection reasonably well, but the downstream link, getting an accurate warning to the right person in time for them to act, remained weak. Cyclone Ditwah\u2019s own retrospective makes this explicit: Sri Lanka was actually among the first countries globally to develop anticipatory-action triggers for a biological hazard like dengue, which shows we\u2019re capable of genuine innovation. But we have noted that pre-positioning of essential supplies based on those triggers materialised only to a limited extent when Ditwah hit, so even where the tools existed, the operational follow-through didn\u2019t fully happen.\n\nQWhy are disaster preparedness and disaster risk mitigation plans and policies important for a country like Sri Lanka?\n\nBecause Sri Lanka sits at a particular intersection of vulnerability that makes preparedness not optional, but foundational."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4",
    "date_str": "2026-08-18",
    "description": "\u201cCyclone Ditwah exposed critical structural gaps in  Sri Lanka\u2019s early warning  architecture\u201d - Prof. Amaratunga -",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Cyclone-Ditwah-exposed-critical-structural-gaps-in-Sri-Lankas-early-warning-architecture-Prof-Amaratunga/334-348330",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787033553.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "69806c39349b4f14e20f02c772d9086b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1804,
    "chunk_text": "QWhy are disaster preparedness and disaster risk mitigation plans and policies important for a country like Sri Lanka?\n\nBecause Sri Lanka sits at a particular intersection of vulnerability that makes preparedness not optional, but foundational.\n\nEconomically, the country simply cannot afford to keep absorbing these losses reactively. Cyclone Ditwah alone affected over two million people and caused damage across every sector such as housing, agriculture, health, and livelihoods, and this was on top of a country still recovering from a severe economic crisis. Post-disaster reconstruction is more expensive than pre-disaster mitigation. Socially, the impact of disasters in Sri Lanka is never evenly distributed. Our research on gender and disaster governance, on post-conflict housing reconstruction, and on vulnerable communities in landslide and flood-prone areas all points to the same pattern: those with the least capacity to absorb shocks, women, the elderly, low-income households, communities in marginal or informal settlements are hit the hardest and recover the slowest. Good preparedness planning is one of the few tools available to correct for that inequity before disaster strikes, rather than trying to patch it afterwards.\n\nAnd institutionally, Sri Lanka has already demonstrated real capability, a functioning Disaster Management Centre, genuine innovation like early anticipatory-action triggers for dengue, and hard-won lessons from the tsunami, the 2016 and 2017 floods, and now Ditwah. That institutional memory and technical capacity is a real asset. But it only pays off if it\u2019s embedded into standing policy and practice, rather than reconstructed reactively after every event. Otherwise we keep re-learning the same lessons at the cost of lives and livelihoods each time."
  },
