    "description": "\u201cCyclone Ditwah exposed critical structural gaps in  Sri Lanka\u2019s early warning  architecture\u201d - Prof. Amaratunga -",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Cyclone-Ditwah-exposed-critical-structural-gaps-in-Sri-Lankas-early-warning-architecture-Prof-Amaratunga/334-348330",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787033553.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4-0005",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c445c7be4897ecc4b32729a43ff194ac",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "And institutionally, Sri Lanka has already demonstrated real capability, a functioning Disaster Management Centre, genuine innovation like early anticipatory-action triggers for dengue, and hard-won lessons from the tsunami, the 2016 and 2017 floods, and now Ditwah. That institutional memory and technical capacity is a real asset. But it only pays off if it\u2019s embedded into standing policy and practice, rather than reconstructed reactively after every event. Otherwise we keep re-learning the same lessons at the cost of lives and livelihoods each time.\n\nRecovery is actually where I think Sri Lanka\u2019s record is most mixed, where genuine achievements sit right alongside recurring, well-documented failures, often in the same reconstruction programme.\n\nOn the achievement side: the sheer scale of post-tsunami housing delivery was real. By the end of 2007, roughly 85% of the required housing programme, around 99,500 of a needed 117,000-plus houses had been completed, which UN-Habitat itself called a genuine success story, particularly along the southern and western coast where implementation went comparatively smoothly.\n\nSome of the community-driven reconstruction models were also genuinely strong, and projects that combined housing with livelihood restoration, skills training for local masons, safer construction techniques, and priority housing for widows and the elderly showed that \u2018build back better\u2019 could be more than a slogan when properly resourced and community-embedded. And critically, 2004 gave us the institutional foundation, the Disaster Management Act, the DMC, that every subsequent recovery has built on, however imperfectly.\n\nOn the failure side, our own work titled \u2018Statement of Research Gaps in Post-tsunami Sri Lanka\u2019 and later research on post-conflict housing reconstruction identified a consistent gap between what recovery plans promised and what was delivered. A few specific, recurring failures stand out:"
  },
