    "date_str": "2026-08-18",
    "description": "\u201cCyclone Ditwah exposed critical structural gaps in  Sri Lanka\u2019s early warning  architecture\u201d - Prof. Amaratunga -",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Cyclone-Ditwah-exposed-critical-structural-gaps-in-Sri-Lankas-early-warning-architecture-Prof-Amaratunga/334-348330",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787033553.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4-0006",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "de3484cbf011b600f1c3973de211b2c8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1716,
    "chunk_text": "On the failure side, our own work titled \u2018Statement of Research Gaps in Post-tsunami Sri Lanka\u2019 and later research on post-conflict housing reconstruction identified a consistent gap between what recovery plans promised and what was delivered. A few specific, recurring failures stand out:\n\nDeep unevenness between regions: Recovery in the south and west proceeded far faster than in the conflict-affected north and east, and housing reconstruction in former conflict zones consistently lagged, which is precisely the fairness and equity failure that our evaluation of \u2018build back better\u2019 in Sri Lanka flagged as a core test of whether the concept was actually being honoured, not just claimed.\n\nThe \u2018transition to what?\u2019 problem: Temporary shelter was frequently disconnected from a clear pathway to permanent, livelihood-connected housing. People moved from emergency shelter into another form of limbo rather than into a functioning, resilient community.\n\nFailure to connect relief with development: Recovery efforts tended to replace what was physically lost without addressing the underlying vulnerability that made communities exposed in the first place. Which is exactly why we then saw major loss of life again in 2016, 2017, and now with Ditwah. If recovery had systematically tackled root vulnerability the first time, later events should have caused proportionally less damage. They didn\u2019t.\n\nGovernance and accountability gaps: Analyses tracking tsunami aid found large gaps between committed and expended donor funds, inaccurate beneficiary lists, and land allocation problems, and structural weaknesses in how recovery was managed and accounted for, not just technical construction issues."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4",
