    "description": "When the risk is judicial independence, a President must not take it",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/When-the-risk-is-judicial-independence-a-President-must-not-take-it/4-796178",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1787036059.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailyftlk-94579078-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "78567e7a9df3be4e4548217546a3f305",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2010,
    "chunk_text": "What they are saying is that the reform must not come at the price of weakening the institution that guarantees the rule of law.\n\nIndeed, it is the position a Government committed to constitutional democracy should take itself.\n\nThe President has said that he is willing to take risks for change.\n\nThe precise constitutional consequences of the present Bill will, of course, depend upon its actual provisions and the constitutional determination applicable to it. But that makes the Government\u2019s responsibility greater, not smaller\n\nBut the relevant question is not whether he is personally willing to bear the risk.\n\nThe President can bear the consequences of an unpopular policy.\n\nBut the consequences of a weakened judiciary are borne by citizens who may one day need an independent court to protect them from the Government itself.\n\nIt belongs within the constitutional structure established to protect the people.\n\nA President should listen when the experts raise a constitutional alarm\n\nThe BASL has opposed the proposal and called for broader consultation. Its meeting with the President on 12 August ended without agreement.\n\nThese voices may not all agree on every constitutional detail. They may have different political perspectives.\n\nA Government that is confident in its case should have nothing to fear from a rigorous public examination.\n\nExplain why the proposed measure is necessary to deal with the backlog.\n\nExplain what safeguards will prevent actual or perceived interference with the judiciary.\n\nInvite the BASL, constitutional scholars, academics, former judges, practicing lawyers and civil society to scrutinise the proposal.\n\nAnd if the Constitution requires the people to decide, let the people decide.\n\nRetreating from a dangerous constitutional course after hearing compelling objections is not defeat.\n\nBut constitutional Government also requires knowing which risks are unacceptable.\n\nIt must not gamble with the institution that stands between the citizen and arbitrary power."
  },
