    "chunk_text": "A: No. There are former senior JVP members, such as Prof. Jayadeva Uyangoda and Prof. Athula Sumathipala, as well as many professional experts, particularly in the expatriate community, who are willing to contribute to building a better and more prosperous Sri Lanka.\n\nI met President Anura Kumara Dissanayake twice before the 2024 Presidential Election. We discussed general policy issues, but there was no invitation to contribute to the party or Government.\n\nQ: If the Government invited you to join in an advisory capacity, would you accept?\n\nA: Yes, depending on the conditions. I am willing to help if I believe I can make a useful and positive contribution. I campaigned for him during the 2024 Presidential Election, travelling to the North, East and other districts to address NPP meetings.\n\nThere are shortcomings in the NPP Government, partly because many of those elected are academics without sufficient ground-level political and administrative experience\n\nQ: Do you think the JVP deviated from its original policies, especially on the national issue, in the 1980s?\n\nA: Yes. The JVP changed significantly after 1983, particularly during 1988-89, from the original policies we developed in the 1970s.\n\nI believe the JVP was responsible for greatly aggravating chauvinism during that period through the Deshapremi Janatha Vyaparaya (DJV). It brought together chauvinist and extremely nationalist organisations, and the effects are still felt in Sri Lankan society. This remains a negative factor in Sri Lankan politics, and the JVP has a responsibility to address it.\n\nQ: Some people also hold the JVP responsible for destroying infrastructure, including power projects, and for killing people during that period. Do you agree?\n\nA: I left the country in September 1989, so I cannot comment on every incident. It was a complex conflict in which different groups committed acts of violence."
