    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "Q: Some people also hold the JVP responsible for destroying infrastructure, including power projects, and for killing people during that period. Do you agree?\n\nA: I left the country in September 1989, so I cannot comment on every incident. It was a complex conflict in which different groups committed acts of violence.\n\nThe fundamental mistake, in my view, was the proscription of the JVP in 1983. If the UNP Government had not banned the party, the JVP might not have taken the path it subsequently followed. But that does not absolve the JVP of responsibility for its actions.\n\nI believe the JVP should have returned to open politics when opportunities arose.\n\nQ: But the party was banned. How could it have come into the open?\n\nA: Even under proscription, it could have used whatever opportunities were available. For example, while I was detained with 17 others, I proposed holding a press conference to explain the JVP\u2019s position. President J.R. Jayewardene had called an all-party conference on the national question, and I suggested that a JVP delegation hold a press conference explaining its position.\n\nQ: You said the JVP was falsely accused of being behind the 1983 riots. Why did the party not openly state its position?\n\nA: We were not involved in the riots, and later we found out which politicians and groups were behind them. Before the riots, I had intended to resign over the JVP\u2019s position on the Tamil issue. We then held the Central Committee meeting on the issue in Tangalle, and the riots broke out soon afterwards.\n\nThe Government banned the JVP, accusing it of being behind the riots, which was not true. I refused Wijeweera\u2019s decision to go underground because we were not involved in the riots. I argued that if we went underground, people would believe the Government\u2019s claim that we were responsible."
