    "chunk_text": "The Government banned the JVP, accusing it of being behind the riots, which was not true. I refused Wijeweera\u2019s decision to go underground because we were not involved in the riots. I argued that if we went underground, people would believe the Government\u2019s claim that we were responsible.\n\nI regard that decision as one of the major mistakes. The leadership could have come into the open, even if it meant being arrested, so that people would understand what had happened.\n\nQ: But later, the then Government wanted to meet the JVP over this issue.\n\nA: Yes. After my release, the JVP Politburo wanted me to meet President Jayewardene because there were no charges against us and requested him to lift the proscription.\n\nI met President Jayewardene at his residence. He said Wijeweera should come and make a statement, after which he would consider lifting the JVP\u2019s proscription.\n\nQ: Why do you think Wijeweera did not take that opportunity?\n\nA: Wijeweera did not want to come out of hiding. I think he feared that he would be harassed or killed.\n\nHe had experienced this fear from childhood. His father had been assaulted by UNP thugs during an election and remained bedridden until his death. I think that may have had a psychological impact on him.\n\nWhen I was with the JVP, whenever there was a problem with the police, they sent me to the police station. Wijeweera would even avoid going near a police station. I think that phobia may have stemmed from the same experience.\n\nQ: There is talk in political circles about friction between the NPP and the JVP. Is it true?\n\nA: I am not aware of the internal politics of the NPP-JVP coalition, so I cannot comment. But I recall the negative experience of internal disputes within the JVP in 1971, which affected the insurrection. By 1983, factions within the JVP had again weakened the party and affected its activities."
