    "chunk_text": "Since 1977, we have become heavily dependent on exports, tourism and migrant remittances. Tea, rubber, coconut, apparel and footwear are important, but these sectors are vulnerable when global conditions deteriorate.\n\nWe have the potential to develop agriculture and renewable energy. Sri Lanka has solar, water, ocean and wind resources that could be better utilised. We also need to reduce our dependence on imported fossil fuels.\n\nI strongly disagreed with the NPP\u2019s proposal to establish a nuclear power plant and have publicly expressed my opposition.\n\nIn terms of priorities, therefore, I do not think the Government has taken all the right initiatives. However, its efforts to tackle corruption and drugs are positive, and I commend them.\n\nQ: Some parties have criticised the Government for inefficiency in handling crucial issues. How do you see this?\n\nA: We have to understand that this is not a socialist Government. It is a social democratic Government operating within a capitalist system, so we cannot expect it to implement socialism.\n\nIt has a parliamentary mandate to pursue a social democratic agenda, and that is what it is attempting to do.\n\nThere are shortcomings, partly because many of those elected are academics without sufficient ground-level political and administrative experience. They are gradually gaining experience, but perhaps not as quickly as we would like.\n\nA: The bureaucracy was developed under previous regimes in a way that discouraged initiative. Officials were generally expected to follow political instructions rather than make independent decisions, and some were also corrupt.\n\nThe same weaknesses remain because there have not been major changes to the administrative system. Some officials may still occupy positions where they can obstruct reforms.\n\nA: Yes. If the Government wanted to retain existing officials, it could have established supervisory and advisory mechanisms to monitor performance against key result areas."
