    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1c7ae2c645e6a9f975057d73523c1c5a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1781,
    "chunk_text": "It is a Friday afternoon at a bank branch somewhere in Colombo and the queue snakes past the pillars and out toward the door. A man near the front counts out cash for a payment he could have sent from his phone in under ten seconds. He doesn\u2019t\u00a0 do things that way, not because the technology isn't available but rather because he didn't realise it was.\n\nAnd yet cash remains king. Small merchants still prefer notes changing hands over a QR code. Salaries get withdrawn in full the day they land. Bank queues persist not because there\u2019s\u00a0 no alternative but because the alternative hasn\u2019t become second nature.\n\nThis is not an infrastructure problem. It is an adoption issue and since trust cannot be legislated into being, adoption issues are more difficult to resolve.\n\nPart of it is simple unfamiliarity. For many consumers, especially those who live outside of major cities and are older, cash is more palpable and understandable than bank transfers. When something goes wrong with cash, you can see the problem immediately. When a digital transfer doesn\u2019t go through, the uncertainty is unnerving: who do you call, how long do you wait, what if the money simply disappears into some invisible queue with no one to explain it?\n\nPart of it is incentive or the lack of it, on the merchant side. A small shop owner weighing the cost of a POS terminal, transaction fees and settlement delays against the simplicity of a cash drawer will often choose the cash drawer. Nothing about digital payments currently tips that calculation clearly in their favour.\n\nAnd part of it is simply awareness. Many account holders have never been shown how CEFTS or LankaPay actually works, what it costs or how quickly it settles. The rails exist but nobody has handed most people the map."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-19-dailyftlk-09d68316",
    "num": "dailyftlk-09d68316",
    "date_str": "2026-08-19",
    "description": "Rails are built: So why are we still queuing?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Rails-are-built-So-why-are-we-still-queuing/4-796216",
