    "description": "Cabinet clears new anti-terror Bill to replace PTA",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Cabinet-clears-new-anti-terror-Bill-to-replace-PTA/56-796228",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1787096665.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-19-dailyftlk-75842633-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9b84807a7a1ae19080f90a381be5d46f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Cabinet clears new anti-terror Bill to replace PTA\n\nCabinet approves Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill for Gazette publication before its presentation to Parliament, following Attorney General approval\n\nLegislation to replace 1979 PTA, addressing longstanding concerns over deficiencies in counter-terrorism and human rights framework\n\nGSP+ is a key consideration, replacing PTA is closely linked to Sri Lanka\u2019s continued eligibility for EU trade concessions\n\nThe Cabinet of Ministers at its meeting on Monday approved the publication of the proposed Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill in the Government Gazette, paving the way for its presentation to Parliament as a replacement for long-standing Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA).\n\nCabinet Spokesperson and Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa told the weekly post-Cabinet meeting media briefing yesterday that the Bill, prepared by the Legal Draftsman, had received clearance from the Attorney General.\n\nThe proposed Protection of the State from Terrorism Act is intended to establish a new legal framework addressing shortcomings identified in the Prevention of Terrorism (Temporary Provisions) Act No. 48 of 1979.\n\nHe said a technical expert committee had earlier been appointed to prepare a preliminary draft to address deficiencies in the existing legislation. Based on its recommendations, the Cabinet granted policy approval on 10 August 2026 to proceed with the new legislation.\n\nThe move comes as Sri Lanka faces continued international scrutiny over its counter-terrorism framework, particularly in relation to human rights and governance commitments linked to the European Union\u2019s (EU) GSP+ trade concessions.\n\nThe GSP+ provides preferential access to the EU market and is particularly important to Sri Lanka\u2019s apparel sector. Eligibility is conditional on the effective implementation of 27 international conventions covering human rights, labour rights, environmental protection and good governance."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-19-dailyftlk-75842633",
    "num": "dailyftlk-75842633",
    "date_str": "2026-08-19",
    "description": "Cabinet clears new anti-terror Bill to replace PTA",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Cabinet-clears-new-anti-terror-Bill-to-replace-PTA/56-796228",
    "lang": "en",
