    "date_str": "2026-08-19",
    "description": "GMOA calls for immediate revision of health sector cadre",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/GMOA-calls-for-immediate-revision-of-health-sector-cadre/108-348397",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787113812.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-19-dailymirrorlk-9522206a-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "563804fc28051d395fe04b535e817262",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1796,
    "chunk_text": "Although the GMOA has repeatedly highlighted the need to revise the approved medical cadre, the process has yet to be completed. The association said the failure to identify the actual workforce requirements has also hindered the recruitment of doctors needed for the future.\n\nIt called for the revision to go beyond an administrative exercise and instead be based on a scientific and analytical assessment with a long-term outlook.\n\nThe association said the revision should consider patient numbers, service volumes, procedures, investigations, the complexity of services and the requirement for 24-hour services. It should also take into account the expansion of health services, newly established institutions and units, and new disciplines and specialist fields introduced over the past decade.\n\nIt further called for vacancies created by the migration of health professionals to be identified and filled, while future challenges, service expansion and structural changes should also be incorporated into the assessment.\n\nThe GMOA stressed that adequate human resources must also be ensured to improve service quality and patient safety.\n\nIt called for the approved cadre of the entire health workforce to be revised immediately through discussions and agreement among relevant stakeholders.\n\nThe association also proposed establishing a mechanism to regularly review approved cadres according to changing service requirements, preventing similar long-term delays in the future.\n\nThe GMOA warned that maintaining sufficient and quality health personnel is essential for the continued stability of Sri Lanka\u2019s free health service, and that failure to revise the approved cadre immediately could pose a serious challenge to the stability of the country\u2019s hospital system in the years ahead."
  },
