    "md5": "a61b8f80f97038294beb2f40e387215a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Freight charges have also increased by 20-30 percent, according to him. Sri Lanka imports most of its essential food items, including pulses, sugar, some varieties of rice, certain fruits, spices, wheat and canned fish.\n\nSri Lanka has also begun to feel the impact of El Ni\u00f1o. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake has established a Special Cabinet Subcommittee and an Officials\u2019 Committee to oversee El Ni\u00f1o preparedness.\n\nAccording to the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO), El Ni\u00f1o conditions are already present and are expected to strengthen through the remainder of 2026, with a 97 percent probability that they will persist into early 2027.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, however, the eventual impact will depend not only on El Ni\u00f1o in the Pacific but also on the Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD), and on how these two climate drivers interact with the monsoons and shorter-term weather systems. Forecasts indicate that a positive IOD may develop alongside El Ni\u00f1o, although there is currently uncertainty regarding its timing and strength, the FAO said.\n\nThis interaction could create a particularly complex risk for Sri Lanka. The Department of Meteorology has indicated that rainfall during July and August may be below normal, particularly in the Dry Zone. From October onward, however, El Ni\u00f1o years are generally associated with above-normal rainfall, while a positive IOD could prolong enhanced rainfall until December.\n\nSri Lanka must therefore prepare not only for drought but also for a possible sequence of water scarcity followed by excessive rainfall, flooding, waterlogging and landslides.\n\nThis matters because national rice availability depends heavily on the performance of individual cultivation seasons. Sri Lanka produced approximately 5.05 million tonnes of paddy in 2025, broadly sufficient to meet most domestic requirements. However, the country does not have an adequate buffer to absorb the failure of a major cultivation season without significant consequences."
