    "chunk_text": "The implementation of the project would also require coordination with several institutions, including the Ministry of Defence, Sri Lanka Air Force, Telecommunications Regulatory Commission, Board of Investment, Colombo Port City Economic Commission, Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority and airport and airline authorities.\n\nOfficials said that telecommunications approvals would be particularly important because the aircraft depend on remote-control and communication technologies.\n\nAccording to the company, less than 900 square metres of space would be needed for take-off and landing. The two-passenger aircraft have 16 propellers and are designed to maintain flight safety even in the event of certain technical failures.\n\nThe aircraft are operated automatically through a central control room using artificial intelligence. They are equipped with 12 battery packs and three flight-control systems are designed to improve stability and safety during emergencies.\n\nThe discussions also focused on the potential use of passenger drones to provide tourists with quicker access to attractions such as Sigiriya and Pidurangala, as well as destinations in Colombo and the Port City.\n\nA proposal was made to establish a model zone in the Port City where the aircraft could initially be operated and evaluated.\n\nThe proposed service is still at the discussion and evaluation stage, and further regulatory, safety and technical assessments will be required before commercial passenger drone operations can begin in Sri Lanka."
