    "chunk_size_bytes": 1738,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cBy signing this pledge, employers are making a public commitment to strengthen disability inclusive employment within their companies. This is a commitment to keep moving forward, to review recruitment practices, remove barriers, engage with workers, and create more opportunities for persons with disabilities,\u201d Simpson said. \u201cReal and lasting progress is only possible through partnership between government, employers, workers, and organizations of persons with disabilities.\u201d\n\n\u201cOur objective is to provide equal opportunities for persons with disabilities through employment, with a special focus on the apparel sector,\u201d Karunarathne said. \u201cThrough community outreach, vocational training, and direct partnerships with employers, we have reached over 17,000 households, trained candidates at our vocational institutes, and supported 12 formal job placements through district-level job fairs. This is a model that works, and one we remain committed to scaling further.\u201d\n\nSecretary General of JAAF, Yohan Lawrence, addressing the gathering, said the inclusion of persons with disabilities in the apparel sector was not a new undertaking, but one built on decades of experience across the industry.\n\n\u201cEmploying persons with disabilities is not an act of philanthropy. It is a sound investment in individuals who are committed to learning the skills of the trade and who often excel in the workplace,\u201d Yohan said. \u201cToo often, persons with disabilities face barriers not because of a lack of ability, but because of limited access and limited opportunity. When employers create inclusive workplaces, they help remove these barriers and allow individuals to contribute their full potential to the national economy.\u201d"
