    "md5": "430ffac2dc511502927f3e3142a7b51c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2040,
    "chunk_text": "Meta\u2019s lawyer Paul Schmidt told the court there was \u201cno dispute\u201d that some social media users face struggles, but that research showed no clear link between \u200cadolescents\u2019 social media use and a lack of wellbeing.\n\nHe also said Mark Zuckerberg, Meta\u2019s cofounder and CEO, shared the company\u2019s desire to improve its services, and not make them dangerous.\n\nIn a statement before the trial, a Meta spokesperson said the states\u2019 claims are unsubstantiated, and the company stands by its record of creating strong protections for teenagers, including launching Instagram Teen Accounts in 2024, which limit who can contact underage users, as well as a feature that allows parents to set time limits on use.\n\nIf Judge Rogers finds Meta liable, she could impose civil penalties and order changes to Facebook and Instagram.\n\nThe potential impact on Meta\u2019s bottom line is existential. The company could face fines as high as $1.4 trillion, which is just shy of its $1.5 trillion market cap. However, the coalition is seeking fines of roughly $200bn.\n\nMeta has already been ordered to pay $942m in fines in a separate New Mexico lawsuit. These include $375m in civil penalties in a March jury verdict and $567m ordered by a judge earlier this month.\n\nMeta has acknowledged that the lawsuits it faces, including those related to youth social media addiction, could lead to \u201csubstantial monetary damages or fines\u201d in a US Securities and Exchange Commission filing in January.\n\nStephanie Otway, a Meta spokesperson, told Al Jazeera in a statement that while the state attorneys general call the trial \u201ca landmark case\u201d, their \u201climited claims are unsubstantiated and their financial demands are vastly disproportionate\u201d.\n\nShe said the attorneys general offer \u201cno proof anyone in their states was misled\u201d and are attempting to \u201cpenalise Meta\u201d for industry-wide challenges like age verification. \u201cRather than sticking to the facts or the law, the states have instead decided to chase an outlandish payout,\u201d she added."
  },
