    "chunk_size_bytes": 1932,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018Guardians\u2019 programme promotes responsible care for street animals\n\nColombo, Aug. 19 (Daily Mirror) - The \u201cGuardians\u201d programme, launched to encourage the public to care for street animals while taking responsibility for their health and welfare, is being introduced as a community-based approach to managing the country\u2019s stray animal population.\n\nThe initiative, launched by the Hakmana Pradeshiya Sabha together with Baw Baw Animal Welfare and the Association of Veterinarians for Humane Management of Animal Population, encourages people who feed community animals to become their \u201cGuardians\u201d.\n\nAccording to the Association of Veterinarians for Humane Management of Animal Population, feeding a hungry animal is an act of compassion, but responsible animal welfare must go beyond providing food.\n\n\u201cThose who feed animals have a responsibility to ensure that they are sterilized, vaccinated against rabies and receive treatment if they become sick or injured,\u201d the Association said.\n\nThe Association also noted that adequate nutrition can increase the reproductive capacity of animals, potentially contributing to an increase in the street animal population.\n\nHowever, it said that this should not be used as a reason to stop feeding animals.\n\n\u201cDo not stop feeding them. These animals are suffering. Please continue to feed them,\u201d the Association said, while emphasizing that feeding should be combined with sterilization, vaccination and responsible monitoring.\n\nUnder the programme, people are encouraged to inform the relevant animal welfare teams when new animals appear at feeding locations or when animals become sick or injured.\n\nThe required services, including sterilization and rabies vaccination, will be provided free of charge, according to the programme organizers.\n\nThe initiative is expected to be further developed with the aim of introducing the \u201cGuardians\u201d programme across Sri Lanka."
