    "md5": "b3924e5095c0b4b7a2599bd5f8f59395",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1795,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. finally breaking the drug mafia\n\nFor decades, Sri Lanka sat at an agonisingly strategic crossroads in the Indian Ocean, acting as a vital maritime trade route and a fertile transit hub for international drug cartels.\n\nThe roots of this national crisis run remarkably deep into our history. During the long years of the LTTE conflict, northern coastal stretches like Velvettithurai, Point Pedro and Mannar were routinely exploited by contraband smuggling networks moving across the narrow waters of the Palk Strait. Over the decades that followed, those early illicit supply lines mutated into a highly organised, multi-million-dollar narcotics trade.\n\nMajor cartel figures operated with near-total impunity, directing street-level distribution in Colombo while lounging in luxury high-rises in Dubai, Bangkok, or Karachi. For years, ordinary Sri Lankans watched a frustrating pattern repeat itself. Local police would parade small street dealers for the news cameras, while the true architects running the syndicates stayed safe overseas.\n\nThat narrative is finally undergoing a sharp and visible shift. Under President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, the government has shown an unprecedented level of political will to dismantle organised crime and its global connections.\n\nWhere previous administrations treated international legal steps as slow, bureaucratic hurdles, the current leadership treats them as direct law enforcement operations. By working closely with Interpol, modernising mutual legal assistance treaties, and sharing real-time intelligence with foreign police forces, Sri Lankan authorities are achieving results once thought impossible. They are actively tracking down high-profile drug lords in foreign nations, arresting them, and extraditing them back to Sri Lankan soil."
  },
  {
