    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ae4bfb8077d9ff609c413e8e27b8ce4b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "Where previous administrations treated international legal steps as slow, bureaucratic hurdles, the current leadership treats them as direct law enforcement operations. By working closely with Interpol, modernising mutual legal assistance treaties, and sharing real-time intelligence with foreign police forces, Sri Lankan authorities are achieving results once thought impossible. They are actively tracking down high-profile drug lords in foreign nations, arresting them, and extraditing them back to Sri Lankan soil.\n\nThis firm strategy deserves recognition because it directly attacks the head of the snake rather than its tail. Arresting a small seller in Maligawatte or Grandpass might clean up a neighborhood street corner for a few days, but extraditing the financier who pays for the entire multi-ton shipment cuts off the supply chain at its true source.\n\nWhile this momentum is commendable, the government must build a comprehensive institutional framework that lasts far beyond a single political term. Winning a permanent war on drugs requires a deeper, long-term policy blueprint.\n\nFirst, law enforcement must focus heavily on financial intelligence and asset recovery. Extraditing kingpins is only the initial step. Stripping them of their luxury properties, bank accounts, shell companies, and vehicle fleets is the second, equally vital step. Money is the lifeblood of organised crime. Until money laundering inside local financial channels is completely eradicated, new criminal figures will always step forward to fill the void left by extradited bosses.\n\nSecond, our northern sea border requires permanent, modern surveillance infrastructure. The historical legacy of the northern smuggling corridor cannot be erased overnight. Providing the Sri Lanka Navy and Coast Guard with advanced radar technology, aerial drones, and joint patrol agreements with Indian maritime authorities is essential."
  },
