    "description": "UNFPA, NYSC train 100 young leaders in disaster preparedness",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/UNFPA-NYSC-train-100-young-leaders-in-disaster-preparedness/108-348439",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787126860.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-19-dailymirrorlk-066a357b-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cfc1317f723882771266493e38cb68cd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1989,
    "chunk_text": "UNFPA, NYSC train 100 young leaders in disaster preparedness\n\nColombo, Aug. 19 (Daily Mirror) - The United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA), in partnership with the National Youth Services Council (NYSC) and under the Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports, recently conducted a national simulation-based programme to strengthen youth participation in disaster preparedness and response.\n\nHeld at the NYSC Auditorium in Maharagama to mark International Youth Day 2026, the programme, titled \u2018The Resilience Lab: Local Youth Action in Crisis Response,\u2019 brought together more than 100 youth leaders from Youth Clubs across the country.\n\nOrganised under this year\u2019s International Youth Day theme, \u2018Different Contexts, Shared Aspirations,\u2019 the initiative aimed to promote a shift from reactive disaster response to proactive preparedness, with a focus on early warning dissemination and strengthening community resilience.\n\nThe programme drew lessons from recent disasters, including Cyclone Ditwah, which affected around 2.2 million people, claimed more than 600 lives and damaged nearly 115,000 homes.\n\nThrough tabletop simulations based on floods, landslides and dengue outbreaks, the participants were trained to make critical decisions during emergencies and strengthen community-level preparedness.\n\nParticular attention was given to ensuring that early warnings reach vulnerable groups, including pregnant women, older persons and persons with disabilities. The exercises also incorporated sexual and reproductive health and rights (SRHR) and gender considerations into emergency response planning.\n\nThe programme was conducted with technical support from the Disaster Management Centre, the Sri Lanka Red Cross Society and the Family Planning Association of Sri Lanka.\n\nSpeaking at the programme, NYSC Chairman and Director General of Youth Affairs Attorney-at-Law Supun Wijerathna said the council\u2019s extensive youth network could play a vital role in responding to disasters."
  },
