    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8f5e159af15c4eada47caf1fa40aad99",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1215,
    "chunk_text": "Speaking at the programme, NYSC Chairman and Director General of Youth Affairs Attorney-at-Law Supun Wijerathna said the council\u2019s extensive youth network could play a vital role in responding to disasters.\n\n\u201cA key objective of this programme is therefore to build proactive youth leadership, not simply young people who respond after a disaster occurs, but young leaders who can help disseminate early warnings, organise communities and take active steps to protect lives,\u201d he said.\n\nUNFPA Sri Lanka Assistant Representative Dr. Dayanath Ranatunga said young people should be viewed not merely as recipients of disaster relief, but as active communicators, first responders and community mobilisers.\n\nHe stressed the importance of ensuring that early warning systems reach vulnerable individuals, including older people living alone, persons with disabilities and pregnant women who may require urgent healthcare.\n\nAccording to 2024 Census data, around 4.78 million people aged between 15 and 29 live in Sri Lanka, accounting for nearly 22% of the country\u2019s population.\n\nThe NYSC network comprises around 14,000 Youth Clubs, providing grassroots access to more than 280,000 young people across the country."
  },
