    "chunk_id": "2026-08-19-dailymirrorlk-285b1669-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "138e1db645cb4a7ebd7b874effe57767",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1139,
    "chunk_text": "The organisation stressed that justice must not only be fair but must also be seen to be fair, warning that extending the terms of sitting judges without a direct decision by the people through a referendum could lead to questions about the perceived impartiality of the Judiciary.\n\nIt said such circumstances could increase pressure on the Judiciary to demonstrate its independence and impartiality and potentially weaken public confidence in the Court.\n\nThe organisation called on lawmakers to exercise the sovereignty of the people in a manner that protects the independence of the Supreme Court and avoids placing the Court in a position where it could be required to hear its own case.\n\nIt also proposed constructive discussions with the Bar Association of Sri Lanka to find a mutually acceptable solution to the issue, saying resolving the divisions that have emerged over the proposed amendment would help the country move forward.\n\nThe NMSJ said it considered it a national responsibility, as a civil society organisation committed to protecting democracy, to bring these concerns to the attention of the Government and the public."
