    "chunk_text": "The High Commissioner said India\u2019s support during Sri Lanka\u2019s 2022 economic crisis, including critical emergency assistance and its role as the first bilateral creditor to provide financing assurances for Sri Lanka\u2019s IMF programme, had also contributed to wider regional stability.\n\nShe also highlighted public goodwill as an important strategic asset, referring to a Pew Research Centre survey published on August 13 which found that 79 percent of Sri Lankans surveyed had a favourable view of India, the highest rating for India among the 36 countries covered by the survey.\n\nColonne identified six priorities for taking the bilateral partnership forward: building mutual trust, strengthening shared prosperity, accelerating connectivity, developing an integrated maritime security partnership, investing in people and exploring opportunities for India and Sri Lanka to jointly contribute to the wider Indian Ocean region.\n\nThe High Commissioner had also addressed the 65th NDC course in August 2025.\n\nFollowing her address and an interactive question-and-answer session, Colonne also met a delegation of NDC officers who are scheduled to visit Sri Lanka from August 31 to September 2 as part of their study tour.\n\nEstablished in 1960, the National Defence College is India\u2019s apex institution for the study of national security and strategy. Sri Lanka has been sending senior officers and officials to the institution for more than five decades"
