    "chunk_text": "CSE extends decline as ASPI slips below 21,450 despite foreign buying\n\nThe Colombo Stock Exchange extended its negative momentum for a second consecutive session on Wednesday, 19 August 2026, with both benchmark indices closing lower amid continued selling pressure across the broader market.\n\nThe All Share Price Index declined by 62.50 points, or 0.29%, to close at 21,416.57, while the S&P Sri Lanka 20 Index fell by 20.52 points, or 0.34%, to end the session at 6,020.38.\n\nThe ASPI opened around the 21,500 level before gradually losing ground throughout the session. Selling pressure intensified during the afternoon, pushing the index below 21,450 before it eventually closed at 21,416.57. The S&P SL20 followed a similar downward trajectory, despite a brief recovery around midday.\n\nTotal market turnover stood at LKR 1.54 billion, with approximately 43.88 million shares changing hands during the session.\n\nThe Food, Beverage and Tobacco sector dominated market activity, generating LKR 461.58 million in turnover from approximately 12.92 million shares. Sunshine Holdings recorded the highest turnover within the sector at LKR 220.61 million.\n\nCrossing transactions contributed approximately LKR 515.07 million, representing around 33% of total equity turnover. The largest crossing was recorded in Seylan Bank, where 2.00 million shares changed hands at LKR 98.80, generating turnover of LKR 197.60 million.\n\nForeign investor activity provided a positive contrast to the weaker market performance, with foreign investors turning net buyers and recording a net foreign inflow of LKR 103.20 million.\n\nMarket breadth nevertheless remained negative, with 72 securities advancing against 128 decliners, resulting in an advance-to-decline ratio of 0.56. This indicated that selling pressure remained relatively broad despite the return of foreign buying."
