    "chunk_size_bytes": 1991,
    "chunk_text": "A year before the Easter massacre and on 22 April 2018 five Muslim lady teachers of Shanmuga Hindu Ladies College, an institution at Trincomalee in the Eastern Province founded in 1923 by Thangamma Shanmugam Pillai in memory of her husband but became a Government institution in 1996, gave notice to its principal that henceforth they would attend the college wearing only the Muslim attire or abaya. This led to protests and counter protests from different ethnic parent groups and finally ended in the court where the said teachers won their case on grounds of human rights. Soon after that this columnist expressed the view in a local newspaper that even institutions have their rights and they expect their rules and norms to be accepted and respected by the staff working in those institutions.\n\nFor over a millennium Muslims in Buddhist Sri Lanka had no issue with their mode of dressing which was not very different from what members of other communities were wearing, and there was undisrupted social cohesion across all local communities.\u00a0 The locally woven coman, kambayam or celai and the imported saree for women, and sarong and veshti for men fitted the cultural ethos of this country and did not disrupt the social cohesion among its component communities.\u00a0 More importantly those dresses suited the climatic condition of tropical Sri Lanka, a basic criterion for any dress worn by any community in any country. Muslim women covered their head with one end of the cloth they wore and during the hot and humid months that dress provided an element of cool comfort.\n\nHowever, the Muslim attire started changing for men in the 1950s because of Tablighi Jamaat influence and for women after the 1980s because of Wahhabi ideological penetration.\u00a0 There were complaints at that time that bearded Muslim male undergraduates in universities acted as spiritual Sartorialists and harassed their female colleagues for not wearing the abaya. Does dress a measure of one\u2019s religiosity?"
