    "md5": "5a1bec5dc2bcbceb2abb80c02eb586bc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1513,
    "chunk_text": "However, the Muslim attire started changing for men in the 1950s because of Tablighi Jamaat influence and for women after the 1980s because of Wahhabi ideological penetration.\u00a0 There were complaints at that time that bearded Muslim male undergraduates in universities acted as spiritual Sartorialists and harassed their female colleagues for not wearing the abaya. Does dress a measure of one\u2019s religiosity?\n\nThe Sri Lanka Muslim Congress (SLMC) and its splinter All Ceylon Makkal Congress (ACMC) are political parties that exploited this alienation to enrich their respective vote banks.\u00a0 Mullahs rallied behind these parties and Muslim cultural issues started occupying the front stage of Muslim political campaigns.\n\nCurrently, when a frustrated political Opposition is trying hard to exploit whatever means available, including issues which could provoke communal tensions to destabilise the ruling Government, the hullabaloo over the abaya is a welcoming addition from Muslim politicians. Incidentally, one of the ironies of this identity politics is that while these politicians are ready to champion the cause of abaya, they don\u2019t show the same commitment in reforming the male-biased Muslim Marriage and Divorce Act.\n\nIt is an indisputable fact of history that no Muslim minority in Asia or for that matter anywhere else had enjoyed over a millennium such a peaceful, magnanimous and hospitable environment as in Buddhist Sri Lanka. Let politics over a piece of cloth not spoil this glorious record."
  },
