    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "Amana Bank 2Q profit rises 33% on financing, trading gains\n\nAmana Bank PLC\u2019s second-quarter profit rose 33 percent, as the growth in its core financing business and a threefold increase in trading income outweighed the higher operating costs and impairment charges.\n\nThe net profit increased to Rs.622.7 million in the three months ended June 30, from Rs.467.2 million a year earlier, according to the bank\u2019s interim financial statements. Earnings per share rose to Rs.1.13, from 85 cents.\n\nThe net financing income climbed 18 percent to Rs.2.23 billion, as the financing income grew faster than the related expenses. The net trading income surged to Rs.275.3 million, from Rs.91.4 million, providing a significant boost to the quarterly earnings.\n\nThose gains lifted the total operating income by 22 percent to Rs.2.84 billion. The net fee and commission income, however, fell 2 percent to Rs.331.4 million, as the commission expenses increased 43 percent.\n\nThe credit costs also rose sharply, with the impairment charges reaching Rs.67.5 million, from Rs.9.5 million a year earlier. The net operating income increased 20 percent to Rs.2.78 billion after accounting for the impairments.\n\nThe operating expenses climbed 15 percent to Rs.1.41 billion, led by the higher personnel, depreciation and other operating costs. Even so, the operating profit before the financial-sector taxes and levies advanced 25 percent to Rs.1.37 billion.\n\nThe pre-tax profit rose 29 percent to Rs.1.03 billion, while the tax charge increased 24 percent to Rs.409.9 million.\n\n\u201cOur performance during the first half of 2026 reflects the continued success of our business model,\u201d Managing Director and CEO Mohamed Azmeer said in the earnings commentary.\n\nross the retail, small and medium-sized enterprise and corporate segments while maintaining the asset quality."
  },
