    "chunk_text": "Govt. shifts export strategy towards higher volumes and scale\n\nThe government is shifting its export strategy towards increasing the volumes and scaling the commercially viable producers, rather than measuring the progress by the number of exporters entering the international markets, Industry and Entrepreneurship Development Minister Sunil Hadunnetti said yesterday.\n\nHowever, the minister did not identify any new incentives, funding commitments or policy measures to support the shift, which comes as the government pursues a steep increase in export earnings over the next four years.\n\n\u201cThe government\u2019s primary target is to increase the export volume rather than merely the number of exporters,\u201d Hadunnetti told the 26th Exporters\u2019 Forum organised by the Export Development Board (EDB).\n\nThe government aims to lift the export earnings to US $ 36 billion by 2030, from a record US $ 17.3 billion in 2025. According to EDB Chairman and Chief Executive Officer Mangala Wijesinghe, achieving that goal would require the export earnings to more than double within five years.\n\nFor 2026, the government is targeting US $ 19 billion in export earnings, an increase of US $ 1.7 billion or nearly 10 percent, from last year.\n\nHadunnetti reiterated that efforts are underway to remove the barriers preventing the rural producers from entering international markets, including the shortcomings in market knowledge, packaging\u00a0and technology.\n\nHe said the exporter constraints were not limited to Colombo and that discussions were being conducted with the state institutions at provincial and district levels.\n\nNo details were provided on how the producers would be financed to invest in technology and packaging or how the government planned to connect them with the overseas buyers.\n\nThe minister also did not specify whether the strategy would include tax concessions, subsidised credit, grants or targeted support for the\u00a0priority industries."
  },
