    "md5": "e84cb7d2ab5db5be0cb4fff9022fbc28",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1760,
    "chunk_text": "Is human thinking at the brink of extinction?\n\nWith Generative AI slowly but subtly commanding its way into search engines and social media apps that are being used by millions of users, one might wonder whether the human brain is forced to stop thinking. A Google search for a simple definition now provides an elaborate explanation, sometimes with related sources. But what is quite funny is the fact that many of these sources often don\u2019t exist.\n\nAccording to an article by Philippa Wain for BBC, second hand book sales have recently increased in an unusual manner. Scores of novels are now being shipped off to faraway warehouses but booksellers are not aware of the final destination. But speculation is rife as to whether these books are being bought to feed the \u2018voracious appetite\u2019 for new information, also known AI.\n\nIn August 2024, three authors Andrea Bartz, Charles Graeber, and Kirk Wallace Johnson sued Anthropic, alleging that the AI company used millions of pirated books from shadow libraries to train its Claude models. The case led to a historic USD 1.5 billion class-action settlement approved in July 2026.\n\nIn 2025, Judge William Alsup ruled that \u2018using books purchased in this way to train AI software was not a violation of US copyright law.\u2019 Court documents also revealed that books were being destroyed in the process of training Anthropic\u2019 AI chatbot, Claude.\n\nEven though booksellers are \u2018happy\u2019 about the spike in book sales, they have expressed dissatisfaction regarding the idea of destroying books. However, documents from Anthropic revealed about a project of ingesting old books known as \u2018Project Panama\u2019. According to the contents, the company aims to \u2018destructively scan all the books in the world.\u2019"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-d75f7f29",
