  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-3d863c70",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3d863c70",
    "date_str": "2026-08-20",
    "description": "Judicial Retirement Age: Let the Judges\u2019 Wishes Guide the Way",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Judicial-Retirement-Age-Let-the-Judges-Wishes-Guide-the-Way/172-348495",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787206639.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-3d863c70-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b5fdd53b5d8cebedf48d45fe61c6cc47",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1299,
    "chunk_text": "Judicial Retirement Age: Let the Judges\u2019 Wishes Guide the Way\n\nOpposition and divergent views have emerged among stakeholders over the Government\u2019s proposal to amend the Constitution and increase the retirement age of judges. While\u00a0 President A K Dissanayake appears determined to proceed, a practical and less contentious solution deserves consideration.\n\nFirst and foremost, why not fill the existing vacancies in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeal without delay? This would immediately strengthen the judiciary and address concerns arising from the present shortage of judges.\n\nSecondly, the Government should ascertain whether the serving judges themselves wish to continue in office beyond the existing retirement age. If a significant number are willing to serve longer, the case for increasing the retirement age could be objectively assessed. If they are not, there would be little justification for pursuing such an amendment.\n\nRather than prolonging the controversy through repeated meetings, protests and counter-protests, the Government should adopt a simple, consultative and pragmatic approach.\n\nLet the needs of the judiciary\u2014and the wishes of its serving judges\u2014determine the way forward, rather than allowing the issue to become a prolonged constitutional confrontation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-c0b54189",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-c0b54189",
    "date_str": "2026-08-20",
    "description": "22A in place of the new Constitution promised",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/22A-in-place-of-the-new-Constitution-promised/172-348496",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787206760.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-c0b54189-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a5cfb00877e2965ae764e3745f68aab4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "22A in place of the new Constitution promised\n\nPresidents Chandrika Kumaratunge,\u00a0 Mahinda Rajapakse and Maithripala Sirisena (left to right) all promised new constitutions but failed to deliver\n\nAs the leaders of the Opposition pointed out, the 22A was not in the manifesto or any other agenda of the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) before the last Presidential or Parliamentary election\n\nThe controversy surrounding the 22nd Amendment to the Constitution has eclipsed almost every political issue in the country, even though ordinary people seem to be totally dissociated with it. They do not seem to be interested in it as if it does not affect their lives.\n\nHowever, it is the main issue and the only issue for those obsessed with politics,\u00a0 and they are making a huge fuss over it. They are so obsessed with it that the government is sneaking in another controversial piece of legislation, \u2018Protection of the State from Terrorism Act (PSTA), to repeal and replace the long-standing and widely criticised Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA) of 1979, without being noticed by the Opposition parties.\n\nInterestingly, the Cabinet granted approval to the justice minister to gazette and present to the Parliament the draft titled \u2018Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill\u2019 on the same day (August 18) the government presented the 22nd Amendment in the Parliament.\n\nAs the leaders of the Opposition pointed out, the 22A was not in the manifesto or any other agenda of the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) before the last Presidential or Parliamentary election. It was a totally new Constitution which would replace the current second republican Constitution, instead piecemeal Constitutional amendments that was promised by the NPP during those elections."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-c0b54189",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-c0b54189",
    "date_str": "2026-08-20",
    "description": "22A in place of the new Constitution promised",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/22A-in-place-of-the-new-Constitution-promised/172-348496",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787206760.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-c0b54189-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e00ab86e42b4fd82bdb7f1724eb0c4a7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1784,
    "chunk_text": "As the leaders of the Opposition pointed out, the 22A was not in the manifesto or any other agenda of the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) before the last Presidential or Parliamentary election. It was a totally new Constitution which would replace the current second republican Constitution, instead piecemeal Constitutional amendments that was promised by the NPP during those elections.\n\nHe contended that adopting a new Constitution is a long process,\u00a0 but some issues that come under the Constitution crop up unexpectedly, demanding immediate or speedy resolution, which is true. The urgency in extending the retirement age of judges, according to\u00a0 leaders of the government, is prompted by the sufferings of the people due to inordinate delay in disposal of court cases. This point has now been ratcheted up by the reports about the huge backlog of unresolved court cases which\u00a0 resulted in\u00a0 prison congestion,\u00a0 apparently causing recent prison riots.\n\nWe are restrained to discuss the contents as well as the merits and demerits of the 22A, as it is now a subject matter of several petitions before the Supreme Court. However, the NPP must have foreseen, prior to the elections, the necessity to bring in amendments to the Constitution before it is replaced with a new one, as several past governments encountered this situation or created such situations.\n\nAlmost all governments since 1970 had promised to repeal the existing Constitutions to adopt new ones. And almost all those governments since 1977 either introduced or attempted to introduce piecemeal amendments before their promises were kept or broken. The first leader to do so was President J.R. Jayewardene who replaced the first Republican Constitution of 1972 with the second Republican Constitution of 1978."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-c0b54189",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-c0b54189",
    "date_str": "2026-08-20",
    "description": "22A in place of the new Constitution promised",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/22A-in-place-of-the-new-Constitution-promised/172-348496",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787206760.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-c0b54189-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0d805eeb8db6ae69a42c62583a1019cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "Almost all governments since 1970 had promised to repeal the existing Constitutions to adopt new ones. And almost all those governments since 1977 either introduced or attempted to introduce piecemeal amendments before their promises were kept or broken. The first leader to do so was President J.R. Jayewardene who replaced the first Republican Constitution of 1972 with the second Republican Constitution of 1978.\n\nIt showed his shaky confidence in the stability of his own government regarding a new Constitution, despite the unprecedented majority it then commanded.\n\nThe prime pledge by Chandrika Kumaratunga -- who broke the 17-year-long rule by the United National Party (UNP) in 1994, first by winning the Parliament election and then by winning the Presidential election -- was to scrap \u201cJ.R. Jayewardene\u2019s \u201cBahubootha\u201d Constitution, as she put it. However, she never did so, but presented a relatively very progressive new draft Constitution in Parliament in 2000. The UNP members set fire to the copies of that draft within the chamber of the Parliament.\n\nShe also attempted to bring in a \u201cfederal\u201d mode of government, as a Constitutional amendment before that, in 1995 and 1996. Then during the \u201cParivasa\u201d (probational) government with the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), the latter compelled her to adopt the 17th Amendment, another progressive step forward.\n\nMahinda Rajapaksa who also promised a new Constitution never attempted to keep it, despite commanding a two thirds power in Parliament. He, instead used that power to strengthen the executive Presidency, which he promised to scrap, through the 18th Amendment.\n\nThis was also true for the Yahapalana government and the Gotabaya Rajapaksa administration. The Maithri-Ranil government, which also promised a new Constitution, instituted a Constitutional Assembly for that end only in January 2016, after adopting the 19th Amendment to the Constitution in April 2015."
  },
  {
