    "chunk_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-6524264d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "aa1815c59025d5ff23e0812ef51d364c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1612,
    "chunk_text": "\u201dAKD lost his revolutionary conscience long ago\u201c Kumar Gunaratnam\n\n\u201cWe are not going to form an alliance with the traditional parties\u201d\n\nWe\u2019re going to introduce new taxes for top capitalists and release the vat and the other taxes imposed on the masses\n\nWe believe in the revolution, and we believe in socialism, and we believe in a system change as well\n\nWe believe that the working class is the main force to do the system change\n\nSri Lankan people, deserve a better society than this, and a better democratic solution for the problems\n\nFormer student leader and the General Secretary of Frontline Socialist Party Kumar Gunaratnam in an interview with Daily Mirror said that there are some contradictions within the students\u2019 unions in the country, but he is hopeful that these unions will come back stronger in the future. He said that President Anura Kumara Dissanayake (AKD) is following the same path that was taken by former Presidents such as Ranil Wickremesinghe and has left is leftist ideologies. He said that AKD has used the JVP brand name to satisfy neoliberalists. Gunaratnam further said that the Party is not worried about the voter base as he firmly believes they will emerge when the suitable time and situation arise.\n\nQ In a rally held early this year, Propaganda Secretary of Frontline Socialist Party Kumar (FSP) stated that it is essential to establish self-governing regions, bicameral legislature consisting of two houses and giving recognition to upcountry Tamils in order to solve the national issue. Don\u2019t you think this itself could lead to many issues in the country?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-6524264d",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-6524264d",
    "date_str": "2026-08-20",
    "description": "\u201dAKD lost his revolutionary conscience long ago\u201c Kumar Gunaratnam",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/AKD-lost-his-revolutionary-conscience-long-ago-Kumar-Gunaratnam/334-348502",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787207511.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-6524264d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d5821c1fb74dfff4cead175ad90bfb74",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "Q In a rally held early this year, Propaganda Secretary of Frontline Socialist Party Kumar (FSP) stated that it is essential to establish self-governing regions, bicameral legislature consisting of two houses and giving recognition to upcountry Tamils in order to solve the national issue. Don\u2019t you think this itself could lead to many issues in the country?\n\nIt doesn\u2019t mean that we can have complete solutions by introducing this, but up to some extent, you can make some decision; if the first does produce any decision, with the majority of the Sinhala parliamentarians, you can stop from the second one. When you talk about the upcountry Tamil people- who came here 200 years ago-they were forcefully brought by the British, imperialist. Their living conditions are poor compared to the other sections of the country\u2019s society. That is a well-known factor. Throughout the years they have been used by different politicians, not only Sinhala politicians, but also the Tamil politicians there. And they have been used; by some of nationalists and chauvinist movements. And we believe that when we consider the national movement in the Northern and Eastern provinces, which had been making demands for decades, they didn\u2019t include the up country Tamil people in their struggle. So we believe that we should find them as a national community. Who have been living here for decades?\n\nTherefore, I don\u2019t think that it\u2019s going to be another problem because this is the reality, but it should have been solved in the past, but most of the politicians who ruled the country, the different parties, and the different names, different residents, use this for their sake. Therefore we, put forward this programme to solve the national issue.\n\nQ\u00a0 Years ago, you were a student leader and your political language has historically included revolution and revolutionary struggles. How do you see student movements at present? Do you think they represent the needs of the hour?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-6524264d",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-6524264d",
    "date_str": "2026-08-20",
    "description": "\u201dAKD lost his revolutionary conscience long ago\u201c Kumar Gunaratnam",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/AKD-lost-his-revolutionary-conscience-long-ago-Kumar-Gunaratnam/334-348502",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787207511.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-6524264d-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0e7e7260f0d1153ba5827b5a7299900e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1643,
    "chunk_text": "Q\u00a0 Years ago, you were a student leader and your political language has historically included revolution and revolutionary struggles. How do you see student movements at present? Do you think they represent the needs of the hour?\n\nIt is not the system change made by the JVP or NPP, but a real system change. We believe that the working class is the main force to do the system change. They are the strata, they are the class that are possessing power. But the student movement in Sri Lanka has been a kind of a radical movement. And they had a great influence on politics; especially when we considered free education for the last four to five decades. I think they had been fighting to secure education for the masses. So in that sense, I think the students\u2019 movement, did a great the job in the past.\n\nBut at present, after introducing this neoliberal economy, that\u2019s not only the economy, but it\u2019s an ideology as well. This has been unfolding from 1977. Now we are facing the outcomes of the neoliberal economy and what\u2019s happening to the society. Today\u2019s society is thinking about themselves and they are not fighting for the others. We have a huge challenge in that regard. I think still they are a strong movement and have influence, but at the same time it has been declining for the last few years.\n\nQ\u00a0 AKD has moved from revolutionary leftist politics to leading a government that works within the existing state, market and international financial system. Do you believe he has betrayed the principles of the Left, or has he simply recognised that those principles cannot govern a modern state in their traditional form?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
