    "md5": "8b126ee640d4fde1781f78f019f1e43d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1701,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka Is the Only South Asian Country Its Neighbors All View Favorably, Pew Survey Finds\n\nColombo, Aug. 20 (Daily Mirror) - A new Pew Research Center survey of public opinion in South Asia has turned up an unusual result: Sri Lanka is the only country in the region that India, Pakistan and Bangladesh all view more favourably than unfavourably. Those three countries rarely agree on much when it comes to each other. On Sri Lanka, they do.\n\nPew researchers Jacob Poushter, Sneha Gubbala and William Miner surveyed 3,566 people in India, 1,039 in Pakistan, 1,044 in Bangladesh and 2,513 in Sri Lanka between February 8 and May 7, 2026. The goal was to measure how the region's largest countries see each other. What came back was a map of old rivalries, with Sri Lanka sitting apart from most of them.\n\nRoughly half of adults in India, Pakistan and Bangladesh view Sri Lanka favourably, and no more than a third in any of the three see it unfavourably. Pakistan (57%) and Bangladesh (54%) rate it warmest. India's view has also improved, up six points since Pew last ran this survey in 2024. No other country in the poll gets the same consistent goodwill from all three of its neighbors.\n\nIndia's numbers split hard along political lines. Seventy-nine percent of Sri Lankans view India favourably, and 70% say they trust Prime Minister Narendra Modi to do the right thing in world affairs. Bangladeshi's are far more divided: only 42% view India favourably, down 15 points since 2024. In Pakistan, that figure drops to 7%.\n\nPakistanis and Bangladeshi's were also the two groups most likely to name India as their country's greatest threat. Indians return the favour: they most often name Pakistan."
  },
