    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "India's numbers split hard along political lines. Seventy-nine percent of Sri Lankans view India favourably, and 70% say they trust Prime Minister Narendra Modi to do the right thing in world affairs. Bangladeshi's are far more divided: only 42% view India favourably, down 15 points since 2024. In Pakistan, that figure drops to 7%.\n\nPakistanis and Bangladeshi's were also the two groups most likely to name India as their country's greatest threat. Indians return the favour: they most often name Pakistan.\n\nIndia is the outlier here as well. Just 8% of Indians view Pakistan favourably, and only a quarter view Bangladesh favourably, a 10-point drop since 2024.\n\nYounger adults, those under 35, tend to view their neighbors more warmly than adults 50 and older. Younger Indians rate Sri Lanka more favourably than older Indians do (54% versus 43%), and are somewhat less negative toward Pakistan (9% favorable versus 5%), though most Indians of every age still view Pakistan unfavourably.\n\nReligion matters too, at least in India. Indian Muslims hold warmer views of Pakistan and Bangladesh than Indian Hindus do: 21% favorable toward Pakistan among Muslims versus 6% among Hindus, and 41% versus 23% on Bangladesh. Even so, two out of three Indian Muslims still view Pakistan negatively. In Sri Lanka, Hindus expressed more confidence in Modi than Sri Lankan Muslims or Buddhists did.\n\nSri Lanka's position matters because of where it sits: a small state that India and China have both tried to pull closer in recent years, through ports, loans and infrastructure deals. Being liked by India, Pakistan and Bangladesh at once suggests Colombo has avoided getting locked into any one country's camp, at least in how ordinary people in those countries see it.\n\nWhether that goodwill counts for anything in trade talks or debt negotiations is a different question. But it's a rare asset in a region where most cross-border views run negative."
