    "md5": "268474cbbdb25067f5ea2c53bf732d6a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1849,
    "chunk_text": "CAA conducts 108 raids on imported cosmetics and footwear\n\nColombo, Aug. 20 (Daily Mirror) - The Consumer Affairs Authority (CAA) conducted 108 special raids islandwide on August 18 and 19 targeting imported cosmetics and footwear, with 84 businesses found unable to produce proper receipts, bills or invoices to establish the source of the goods.\n\nThe raids focused on imported skin-lightening products, face and body cosmetics, perfumes, hair-care products, sports shoes and other imported footwear.\n\nAccording to the CAA, officers found several businesses where no bills or invoices were available, suppliers could not be identified and the manner in which the goods had entered the shops could not be established.\n\nElectronic goods valued at around Rs. 1.5 million were seized during the raids, while legal action has been initiated against the relevant traders. Officers have also been instructed to continue investigations into businesses where the source of imported goods cannot be verified.\n\nMeanwhile, the CAA said around 785 raids had been conducted this year in relation to creams and lotions, with cases filed against the relevant traders.\n\nUnder Order No. 83 issued by the CAA, manufacturers, suppliers, distributors, warehouse operators and traders who sell, possess or control goods are required to maintain written or printed bills or invoices establishing how the goods were obtained.\n\nSuch documents must contain details including the supplier's name and address, date of purchase, purchase price, type and quantity of goods and, where applicable, the batch number. These records enable authorities to trace the supply chain of products.\n\nThe CAA said the requirement goes beyond simply keeping a bill, as identifying the supplier, importer and other responsible parties is essential for market regulation and consumer protection."
