    "chunk_size_bytes": 1392,
    "chunk_text": "Such documents must contain details including the supplier's name and address, date of purchase, purchase price, type and quantity of goods and, where applicable, the batch number. These records enable authorities to trace the supply chain of products.\n\nThe CAA said the requirement goes beyond simply keeping a bill, as identifying the supplier, importer and other responsible parties is essential for market regulation and consumer protection.\n\nThe Authority also stressed that such raids are intended to protect businesses operating legally and complying with regulatory requirements. Lawful importers bear costs related to duties, taxes, fees, documentation and other regulatory obligations.\n\nHowever, goods entering the market through informal supply channels can make it difficult to verify their quality, safety, standards, manufacturer or importer. The sale of potentially substandard products at lower prices could harm consumers while placing businesses that comply with the law at a disadvantage.\n\nThe CAA said the purpose of the special raids is therefore not merely to increase the number of cases filed, but to ensure a fair trading environment, protect consumers and promote fair competition.\n\nThe Authority said it will continue conducting special raids and market investigations targeting imported cosmetics, footwear and other selected products where similar concerns arise."
