    "md5": "75a7eee329581150cf310f59ca575ed7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "What options is Iran left with? To retaliate. This is the stance of hardliners, especially the Revolutionary Guard leadership, determined to punish the US for starting the war and killing the highly respected supreme leader. If Iran is not allowed to export oil, none in the region will. If Iran suffers economic harm, US allies in the region should also endure similar pain. Such a stance threatens to widen the war.\n\nModerates in Iran, however, insist on continuing the talks based on the MoU mediated by Pakistan and Qatar. Former Iranian President Mohammad Khatami this week questioned the hardliners\u2019 stance that Iran\u2019s \u2018life and survival lie in war\u2019.\n\n\u201cSome say, \u2018First, we will teach America a lesson here, then we will go to the heart of America and fight it there.\u2019 But what reality is this based on?\u201d he asked.\n\n\u201cMy feelings were also wounded when they killed our commander, and one\u2019s eyes could almost weep blood. But governing a society cannot be based on raw emotion,\u201d he added. \u201cWe must consider what serves the ultimate interests of the country, the system, and Islam.\u201d\n\nIran\u2019s President Masoud Pezeshkian is also pushing for talks and peace. Last week, he called for an end to what he described as a state of \u2018neither war nor peace\u2019 with the US. He said Iran was now in a position of strength, and hence it was the right time to reach an agreement with Washington.\n\nModerates argue Iran should not overstretch its advantage over the Strait of Hormuz, noting that networks of pipelines can diminish the waterway\u2019s importance.\n\nThese differences of opinion do not amount to serious cracks in Iran\u2019s body politic, but the moderates\u2019 view reflects public opinion. The hardliners will find it difficult to ignore it, especially if economic wounds deepen.\n\nBoth the US and Iran should realise that a give-war-a-chance approach aimed at exhausting the other also has its limits."
  },
