    "md5": "9aa1cb5e82c20d03052ff30138f198eb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1411,
    "chunk_text": "El Nino: a call for climate resilient agriculture\n\nAs global weather patterns grow increasingly chaotic under the influence of shifting oceanic temperatures and intensifying El Ni\u00f1o phenomena, Sri Lanka, like many other countries, is at a perilous agricultural crossroad. Recent reportage in the Daily Mirror paints a troubling picture of the impending weather duality expected to impact the island\u2019s upcoming farming cycles: a Maha cultivation season fraught with intense, highly localised heavy rainfall, followed immediately by a severely dry, prolonged \u2018Yala\u2019 season marked by heat waves and acute drought.\n\nCompounding this domestic uncertainty, recent global economic reports, including comprehensive assessments from the World Bank, warn that climate disruptions, coupled with ongoing supply chain bottlenecks, threaten to drive regional\u00a0 food import prices up significantly.\n\nFor a country whose smallholder farmers produce the bulk of domestic food supply, these looming climate shocks are not mere abstract meteorological forecasts\u2014they are existential threats to national food security, macro-economic stability, and rural livelihoods.\u00a0 Climate adaptation is no longer a peripheral policy or a post-disaster afterthought. Sri Lanka must urgently accelerate its transition toward climate-resilient agriculture, converting seasonal emergency responses into a permanent, modernised framework."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
