    "description": "El Nino: a call for climate resilient agriculture",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/El-Nino-a-call-for-climate-resilient-agriculture/172-348540",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787269586.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-21-dailymirrorlk-858688b1-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0b1455c7f0c0088da85400b0307b4b21",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1895,
    "chunk_text": "For a country whose smallholder farmers produce the bulk of domestic food supply, these looming climate shocks are not mere abstract meteorological forecasts\u2014they are existential threats to national food security, macro-economic stability, and rural livelihoods.\u00a0 Climate adaptation is no longer a peripheral policy or a post-disaster afterthought. Sri Lanka must urgently accelerate its transition toward climate-resilient agriculture, converting seasonal emergency responses into a permanent, modernised framework.\n\nThis is where institutional alignment and strategic investment become paramount. Frameworks highlighted in World Bank agricultural assessments point to a clear roadmap for developing countries facing extreme weather vulnerability. Building an agri-food sector capable of withstanding severe climate volatility demands rapid, scaled investment across three foundational pillars: adaptive water infrastructure, precision technology, and targeted economic safety nets for smallholders.\n\nFirst, water resource infrastructure must be modernised to function as dynamic, two-way buffers. Sri Lanka\u2019s ancient cascade tank systems provided historical resilience, but contemporary climate extremes require upgraded irrigation technology. Storage systems must be capable of absorbing sudden, heavy volumes of water during torrential deluges while minimising evaporation and wastage during months of extreme heat.\n\nSecond, the state and the private sector must accelerate the adoption of digitalised, precision agriculture. While promoting climate-resilient seed varieties\u2014such as flood-tolerant paddy and high-yield, short-maturity pulses\u2014is essential, it must be paired with localised, tech-enabled extension services. Equipping smallholders with real-time micro-weather data, soil moisture sensors, and automated pest prediction models empowers them to make informed decisions."
  },
