    "language": "en",
    "md5": "807b737808a7a07262f881f4508411e7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1464,
    "chunk_text": "Second, the state and the private sector must accelerate the adoption of digitalised, precision agriculture. While promoting climate-resilient seed varieties\u2014such as flood-tolerant paddy and high-yield, short-maturity pulses\u2014is essential, it must be paired with localised, tech-enabled extension services. Equipping smallholders with real-time micro-weather data, soil moisture sensors, and automated pest prediction models empowers them to make informed decisions.\n\nAddressing the threat posed by El Ni\u00f1o also demands strong political will and institutional cohesion. While high-level task forces and ministry advisories demonstrate growing policy awareness, bureaucratic inertia often slows implementation at the field level. Agrarian services, irrigation authorities, local government bodies, and farmer organisations must operate under a unified, streamlined mandate.\n\nSri Lanka cannot afford to wait for reservoir levels to plummet or crop fields to wither before committing to structural change. Relying heavily on global food markets during periods of climate instability is a high-risk strategy. The imminent threat of El Ni\u00f1o must serve as an unyielding catalyst for structural reform. By combining local agronomic heritage with modern technology, strategic infrastructure investments, and robust policy execution, Sri Lanka can shield its agricultural sector from climate shocks\u2014transforming acute vulnerability into enduring national resilience."
  },
