    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "89bb9a7513f25e4e6f1f60f4106a0959",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "LOLC 1Q earnings rise as portfolio drag eases\n\nLOLC Holdings PLC reported a 34 percent increase in first-quarter (1Q) profit attributable to shareholders, as higher income and a sharp reduction in losses across several non-financial businesses outweighed rising impairment charges and weaker profitability in its core financial services segment.\n\nProfit attributable to equity holders rose to Rs. 6.18 billion in the three months ended June 30, from Rs. 4.63 billion a year earlier, according to the diversified group\u2019s unaudited interim financial statements. Earnings per share increased to Rs. 13 from Rs. 9.74.\n\nOverall Group profit for the quarter more than doubled to Rs. 4.76 billion from Rs. 2.37 billion, with the year-earlier period including a Rs. 483 million loss from discontinued operations. Profit from continuing operations increased 67 percent to Rs. 4.76 billion.\n\nNon-controlling interests recorded a Rs. 1.41 billion loss, compared with a Rs. 2.02 billion loss from continuing operations a year earlier.\n\nGross income climbed 34 percent to Rs. 123.44 billion, while net interest income rose 26 percent to Rs. 34.75 billion. Revenue from the Group\u2019s non-financial operations increased 33 percent to Rs. 43.13 billion, helping gross profit from those activities rise 38 percent to Rs. 18.34 billion.\n\nThe improvement at the top line did not translate proportionately into operating earnings as costs accelerated. Personnel expenses rose 24 percent to Rs. 20 billion, while other operating expenses jumped 49 percent to Rs. 16.91 billion. Impairment losses on financial assets increased 61 percent to Rs. 8.65 billion from Rs. 5.36 billion.\n\nAs a result, operating profit grew at a slower 22 percent to Rs. 19.88 billion. Net finance costs eased 3 percent to Rs. 12.58 billion, helping profit before tax from continuing operations surge 78 percent to Rs. 7.58 billion. The tax charge more than doubled to Rs. 2.82 billion."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
