    "chunk_size_bytes": 1935,
    "chunk_text": "CTC profit expands in 2Q even as turnover contracts\n\nCeylon Tobacco Company PLC (CTC) posted a 4.5 percent increase in second-quarter (2Q) net profit despite weaker turnover and revenue, as lower operating expenses and a reduced tax charge helped offset rising raw material and employee costs.\n\nProfit for the three months ended June 30 rose to Rs.7.69 billion from Rs.7.37 billion a year earlier, according to the company\u2019s interim financial statements. Earnings per share increased to Rs.41.07 from Rs.39.32.\n\nThe bottom-line improvement came even as the cigarette maker\u2019s top line contracted. Turnover fell 4.9 percent year-on-year to Rs.52.44 billion from Rs.55.14 billion, while revenue after government levies declined 2.4 percent to Rs.16.57 billion from Rs.16.98 billion.\n\nThe divergence between revenue and earnings reflected a sharp reduction in other operating expenses, which dropped 43.9 percent to Rs.1.03 billion from Rs.1.84 billion. That provided a buffer against significantly higher input and staff costs.\n\nRaw material costs jumped 37.5 percent to Rs.913 million, while employee benefit expenses surged 50.3 percent to Rs.1.14 billion. Depreciation and amortisation also increased to Rs.180 million from Rs.129 million. Even with the operating cost savings, pre-tax profit slipped 1.8 percent to Rs.13.62 billion from Rs.13.88 billion. The improvement at the net-profit level was therefore driven partly by taxation, with the income tax charge falling 8.9 percent to Rs.5.93 billion from Rs.6.51 billion a year earlier.\n\nCTC said it maintained its revenue performance against a challenging macroeconomic environment and pointed to savings initiatives and cost controls as supporting profitability during the quarter. For the first six months, profit increased 2.5 percent to Rs.14.39 billion, while revenue rose 4.8 percent to Rs.32.64 billion. First-half pre-tax profit climbed 4.4 percent to Rs.26.62 billion."
