    "chunk_text": "Tradition, text, and lived realities:  Anthropological engagement with Wimal Dissanayake\u2019s cinema studies\n\nThe recent passing of Prof. Wimal Dissanayake marks a major transition in post-colonial cultural scholarship, making an anthropological engagement with his extensive legacy both timely and necessary (Newswire.lk 2026; Dissanayake 1939\u20132026). An objective assessment of his work requires evaluating his framework in Enabling Traditions (2005), which argues that heritage is not a stagnant, regressive obstacle to modernisation, but a dynamic, generative tool for indigenous identity and intellectual autonomy. When extended to his critiques of Indian and Sri Lankan cinema, this thesis provides a compelling methodology for reading films as active cultural texts. However, from the perspective of cultural anthropology, Dissanayake\u2019s non-anthropological background reveals an enduring tension: his text-based model of \u201cenabling traditions\u201d frequently clashes with the complex, lived realities of vernacular folk life.\n\nDissanayake\u2019s critical contributions successfully challenge Eurocentric hegemony within film analysis. For decades, Western-trained scholars evaluated Asian visual culture through universalising lenses like Hollywood continuity editing or psychoanalysis. Dissanayake disrupts this by activating classical Eastern poetics, utilising the Sanskrit concepts of Rasa (aesthetic flavor) and Dhvani (suggestion) to decode the works of Satyajit Ray and Lester James Peries (Dissanayake 2003)."
  },
