    "md5": "2f2f2a4564e7fc61990f76c873066b78",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "As cultural anthropologists of the region have long observed, prioritising Brahmanical or highly Sanskritised textual models to explain modern creative practices inevitably replicates the ideological biases of elite, literate classes (Dirks 2001). By filtering visual culture exclusively through classical epics or Rasa theory, Dissanayake\u2019s framework inadvertently glosses over the complex socio-political fractures of caste and gender that dictate how communities actually experience social space.\n\nFurthermore, materialist and political-economic critiques reveal that Dissanayake\u2019s focus on text and cognitive coping mechanisms causes him to undertheorise the concrete realities of cultural production. While he reads family melodrama as an abstract allegory for resisting Westernisation, he downplays the industrial and institutional constraints governing visual culture. A community\u2019s artistic output is never entirely autonomous; it is aggressively restricted by corporate financing, state censorship, and market distribution networks. By prioritising what the cinematic text signifies, his framework isolates the cultural product from the material conditions of its manufacturing (Banaji 2006).\n\nFinally, a major paradox exists within his method of \u201cDisruptive Inquiry.\u201d While Dissanayake fiercely critiques Western models for their Eurocentrism, his own analytical toolkit remains deeply dependent on Western critical theorists like Walter Benjamin and the Frankfurt School to validate his Eastern frameworks. For an anthropologist attuned to unmediated vernacular voices, this raises a poignant question: does this approach achieve genuine local intellectual autonomy, or does it merely repackage Eastern heritage into a specialised idiom tailored for Western academic consumption (Guneratne and Dissanayake 2003; Silva 2016)?"
