    "chunk_size_bytes": 1826,
    "chunk_text": "Tribute to Sir Christopher Ondaatje \u2013 Diagonal journey across Sri Lanka\n\nSir Christopher Ondaatje surveying Hambantota Bay from the lawn of the Hambantota Resthouse, where he remarked that it was \u201cprobably the place that brings back most memories\n\nSir Christopher Ondaatje was an enigmatic and illustrious figure, remembered as a financial magnate, a passionate explorer, a celebrated author, and perhaps the greatest \u2018Global Ceylonese\u2019. Born in Ceylon on 22nd February 1933, he left his homeland in 1947 to attend school in England. After spending five years in the City of London, he moved to Canada, where he built a vast fortune\u2014first in finance, and later in publishing. At the height of his financial power, he bravely sold all his companies to return to his first passions: writing and exploration. His passing on 20th July 2026 marks the end of an era, but his profound legacy endures through his extensive literature, his philanthropy, and his devoted family.\n\nI first met Christopher in 2004 when I was in London. He comes from the famous Ondaatje family. He has written a number of books, but the book closest to his heart was The Man-Eater of Punanai (1992), which recounts the tale of a man-eating leopard that terrorised the tiny village of Punanai in the early 20th century until an English tea planter, Shelton Agar, shot it.\n\nFar from being only the story of this cunning leopard, Ondaatje uses the narrative of his return to Sri Lanka after thirty-eight years to pick up the pieces of his early family life. He uses the story of the man-eater as a metaphor for his relationship with his tyrannical but much-loved father. It is a classic parable and love story that has sold over a hundred thousand copies, describing a way of life that has now completely disappeared on the island he loved so much."
  },
