    "md5": "142f7ba564083100b3f1391a1d90443a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1700,
    "chunk_text": "The rationale for the Mecca Pact and its spinoffs\n\nA defence pact was signed in Mecca by Turkiye, Saudi Arabia and Pakistan on August 7\n\nThe defence pact signed in Mecca by Turkiye, Saudi Arabia and Pakistan on August 7, stemmed partly from a growing unease with the stance of the United States on security issues in West Asia and partly from a recognition that the US, under President Donald Trump, wishes to lessen its involvement in West Asia and concentrate more on the Russian threat to Europe and China\u2019s threat to Taiwan and the South China Sea.\n\nBut the Mecca Pact has other consequences. Israel\u2019s relations with the US and India will be strengthened, and India\u2019s relations with Pakistan and Turkiye will further deteriorate.\n\nArab countries which had handed over their security to the US and given it bases on their soil, felt let down when Washington seemed more interested in defending and encouraging Israel\u2019s aggression against other countries in the region than in protecting its Arab allies from a well-equipped and aggressive Iran.\n\nIt seemed to the signatories of the Mecca Pact and other countries in the region that the US had begun to use Israel as its satrap in the Middle East to carry out its policies for the region.\n\nThe US\u2019s emerging attitude to West Asia suits Israel as it will have the freedom to carry out aggression in Gaza, Lebanon, Iran or wherever it wants while its access to American funds and armaments remains unchanged.\n\nAn Israel that is left free to do what it wants will be a permanent threat to every country in the Middle East. It is to defend themselves from a rogue State, that Saudi Arabia, Turkiye and Pakistan have forged the Mecca alliance."
  },
