    "chunk_size_bytes": 1756,
    "chunk_text": "More recently, at an industry exhibition held in Pakistan with representatives from 32 institutions in the two countries, Turkish Defence Minister Ya\u015far Guler said an official agreement for Pakistani participation in the development and production of the KAAN fighter jet was close to completion. The relationship became particularly significant during the India-Pakistan confrontation in May 2025, when Turkish drones were deployed.\n\nThe UAE, which has watched the expansion of Turkish influence in the Middle East with concern, particularly because of Ankara\u2019s links to political Islamist movements including the Muslim Brotherhood, is likely to find itself at the centre of a new source of friction with Riyadh, Birvadker says.\n\nThe Emirates, it should be recalled, was the leading Arab state to break with the long-standing regional consensus and establish formal relations with Israel under the 2020 Abraham Accords. Since then, Abu Dhabi has further deepened and expanded its strategic and defence cooperation with Israel, despite public criticism from senior Emirati officials over Israel\u2019s fighting in Gaza and Lebanon.\n\nAlthough the UAE imports fewer weapons than Saudi Arabia, it nevertheless ranks among the world\u2019s major arms buyers, reflecting a clear strategy: \u201csmall but lethal.\u201d Abu Dhabi\u2019s policy of diversifying its suppliers, born of necessity during the Obama administration, led it to deepen its strategic and defence partnership with Israel while rapidly strengthening its alignment with India. Cooperation with New Delhi has since moved to a new level with the signing of a strategic framework agreement, culminating in a multibillion-dollar deal now taking shape for the acquisition of BrahMos missiles, Birvadker says."
  },
  {
