    "chunk_size_bytes": 1595,
    "chunk_text": "Although the UAE imports fewer weapons than Saudi Arabia, it nevertheless ranks among the world\u2019s major arms buyers, reflecting a clear strategy: \u201csmall but lethal.\u201d Abu Dhabi\u2019s policy of diversifying its suppliers, born of necessity during the Obama administration, led it to deepen its strategic and defence partnership with Israel while rapidly strengthening its alignment with India. Cooperation with New Delhi has since moved to a new level with the signing of a strategic framework agreement, culminating in a multibillion-dollar deal now taking shape for the acquisition of BrahMos missiles, Birvadker says.\n\nStrategic relations between Israel and India, which flourished under the leadership of Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi and produced diplomatic backing, military assistance and an expansion of production lines, are emerging as a critical component of Israel\u2019s national resilience in the face of the threat of arms embargoes and international isolation, Birvadker says.\n\nThe UAE, India and Israel have been bound by overlapping interests, close relations and a deep foundation of mutual trust. That makes such an alliance an essential geopolitical anchor for stabilising the region and containing the threats now taking shape, according to Birvadker.\n\nThus, the Mecca Pact does not take West and South Asia toward peace. It ensures security for some and causes insecurity among others. Far from resolving conflicts, it strengthens some countries against perceived aggressors, who in turn are encouraged to beef up their defences."
