    "description": "Hayleys Advantis-National Council for Road Safety launch \u2018100 for 100\u2019 rider safety initiative",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Hayleys-Advantis-National-Council-for-Road-Safety-launch-100-for-100-rider-safety-initiative/215-348643",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787381202.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-22-dailymirrorlk-283f3b6b-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2f83b6f7b97ad5bf4ca636c3425a69b5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1609,
    "chunk_text": "Under the programme, each participating organisation will identify 100 riders from among its employees, operational riders, delivery partners or regular motorcycle commuters. These riders will receive defensive riding education and related safety guidance covering areas such as hazard anticipation, road discipline, responsible riding behaviour, helmet and personal protective equipment awareness and crash prevention.\n\nHayleys Advantis Group Managing Director Ruwan Waidyaratne said, \u201cMotorcycle riders are a large and varied group of road users but unlike many other vehicle categories, there is no single network through which we can consistently reach them with road safety education and training. We saw an opportunity to use workplaces as practical touchpoints to reach riders within their own networks. This led us to develop \u2018100 for 100\u2019 in collaboration with the NCRS, enabling organisations to take an active role in promoting safer riding. We hope to see organisations across the country take up the challenge and help us build a movement for safer riding across Sri Lanka.\u201d\n\nNCRS Chairman Manjula Kularatne stated, \u201c\u2018100 for 100\u2019 is a strong example of how the private sector can complement national road safety efforts through practical action. We welcome the collaboration with Hayleys Advantis and encourage more organisations to come forward, engage their riders and become active partners in creating safer roads across Sri Lanka. When the government, businesses and communities work together, we can extend the reach of road safety initiatives and create a lasting impact.\u201d"
  },
