    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "36581fe28cb160e2baa9de7411da876a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "The Sri Lankan exporters have the advantage of the India Sri Lanka Free Trade Agreement (ISFTA), which has provided preferential market access since 2000. In 2025, approximately US $ 634 million or 61.3 percent of Sri Lanka\u2019s exports to India, were exported under the ISFTA framework.\n\nA key feature of the NCE delegation is the strong network of Indian chamber partnerships established to facilitate direct business engagement.\n\nIn New Delhi, the delegation will engage with the Federation of Indian Export Organisations (FIEO) and Federation of Indian Chambers of Commerce and Industry (FICCI).\n\nIn Mumbai, the participants will engage with the Maharashtra Chamber of Commerce, Industry and Agriculture (MACCIA), All India Association of Industries (AIAI) and International Chamber of Investment and Business (ICIB).\n\nThese partner organisations will facilitate B2B meetings with their members, providing the Sri Lankan companies with opportunities to meet potential buyers, importers, distributors, investors and other business partners.\n\nThe delegation is also being supported by the Sri Lankan High Commission in New Delhi and Consulate General of Sri Lanka in Mumbai, with dedicated trade officers assisting in facilitating the programme and strengthening engagement with the Indian business community.\n\nFor many Sri Lankan companies, particularly the SMEs and emerging exporters, accessing a market as large and diverse as India can be challenging without the right contacts and market knowledge. The NCE delegation is designed to provide a structured platform to overcome this barrier.\n\nThe participants will have the opportunity to assess market demand, meet potential business partners, explore export and distribution opportunities, identify joint-venture and investment possibilities and gain first-hand insights into the Indian market."
  },
