    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8aaab09ab336cebd21cebb46e7d862b5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "Transport was the largest source of annual non-food inflation, contributing 2.23 percentage points, followed by housing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels at 1.21 percentage points. Restaurants and hotels contributed 0.50 percentage points, education 0.35 percentage points and miscellaneous goods and services 0.26 percentage points.\n\nThe overall NCPI increased just 0.5 percent from June, with the non-food prices accounting for 0.42 percentage points of the increase and food contributing 0.08 percentage points.\n\nWithin the non-food expenditure, the higher housing rents were a key driver, contributing 0.28 percentage points to the monthly index increase. The bus fares added 0.15 percentage points, while the secondary tuition fees contributed another 0.06 percentage points.\n\nThose increases were partly offset by the lower fuel costs. The petrol prices reduced the monthly index by 0.19 percentage points, while the LP gas and diesel made negative contributions of 0.14 percentage points and 0.04 percentage points, respectively.\n\nThe higher prices of green chillies made the largest upward food contribution at 0.24 percentage points, followed by dried fish at 0.12 percentage points and milk powder at 0.05 percentage points. Eggs contributed another 0.04 percentage points.\n\nHowever, these increases were largely countered by the falling prices of several staples. Vegetables reduced the index by 0.30 percentage points, while the coconuts lowered it by 0.11 percentage points. The prices of limes, rice and Mysore dhal each made negative contributions of 0.03 percentage points.\n\nOn a year-on-year basis, the food category nevertheless showed a clear acceleration, with inflation rising to 4.9 percent in July, from 3.3 percent in June. Non-food inflation eased only marginally to 9.2 percent, from 9.3 percent, leaving it substantially above food inflation."
  },
