    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "Fire at Anawilundawa Ramsar Wetland brought under control; Probe underway\n\nColombo, August 22 (Daily Mirror) - A sudden fire that broke out at the Anawilundawa Ramsar Wetland Sanctuary has been brought fully under control, with the damage limited to approximately eight acres, the Ministry of Environment said.\n\nEnvironment Deputy Minister Anton Jayakody inspected the affected area today (22) and held discussions on the damage caused and measures required to strengthen the conservation of the wetland.\n\nThe fire had spread for nearly two days amid dry weather and strong winds. Following alerts raised by several parties, including the Chairman of the Arachchikattuwa Pradeshiya Sabha, the Government launched immediate measures to contain the blaze.\n\nWith the assistance of Deputy Minister of Defence Aruna Jayasekara, Sri Lanka Air Force helicopters equipped with Bambi Buckets were deployed to drop seawater over the affected areas.\n\nThe Wildlife Conservation Department, Sri Lanka Police, the Divisional Secretariat and local residents also joined the firefighting efforts, preventing the fire from causing widespread damage to the wetland, which covers more than 3,000 hectares.\n\nA special investigation is underway to determine whether the fire was deliberately set or involved any act of sabotage.\n\n\u201cIf this was intentional or an act of sabotage, maximum legal action will be taken against those responsible,\u201d Deputy Minister Jayakody said, stressing that strict action would be taken against anyone found responsible for unlawful destruction of the environmentally sensitive area.\n\nThe Deputy Minister also said steps would be taken to urgently reconstruct the bund of the Anawilundawa reservoir, which was breached following Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nWith technical assistance from national expert institutions and engineers, the Government plans to restore the reservoir before the upcoming monsoon season."
