    "md5": "0edf4a44e9435e7a53b85b484ec777c2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "Namal urges President to act on allegations involving Presidential Secretary\n\nColombo, August 22 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka Podujana Peramuna (SLPP) National Organiser and Parliamentarian Namal Rajapaksa has called on President Anura Kumara Dissanayake to take appropriate action over allegations concerning the Presidential Secretary, saying such action would demonstrate the Government\u2019s commitment to transparency and accountability.\n\nAddressing a public meeting in Kuliyapitiya today (22), he said the President had repeatedly spoken about combating corruption and urged him to demonstrate that commitment by addressing the allegations.\n\n\u201cThe President speaks about catching thieves and ensuring honesty. This is an opportunity to demonstrate that commitment. We are waiting to see what action will be taken regarding the allegations concerning the Presidential Secretary,\u201d he said.\n\nRajapaksa raised concerns about the Government\u2019s progress in fulfilling several promises made to farmers, graduates and young people during the election campaign.\n\nHe said farmers were facing difficulties due to low paddy prices, while consumers were being affected by high rice prices.\n\nHe also expressed concern over the tax burden on the public and rising electricity and fuel costs, claiming that businesses, particularly young entrepreneurs, were finding it increasingly difficult to operate under the current economic conditions.\n\nRajapaksa also raised concerns about the Government\u2019s handling of alleged security and cyber incidents involving state institutions and called for proper investigations to identify those responsible.\n\nHe said the Government should focus on addressing the concerns of farmers, graduates, young people and businesses rather than engaging in political disagreements.\n\n\u201cThe Government should set aside confrontation and focus on working for the people. The public expects solutions to their problems, not further political disputes,\u201d he said."
