    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dfaab072f4f720542915c29ea422d2cf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1993,
    "chunk_text": "Deloitte report highlights NSBM\u2019s decade of national impact\n\nDeloitte FAS (Pvt.) Ltd study highlights NSBM Green University\u2019s economic, social and environmental impact over the past decade, during which it has served more than 41,000 students, produced 22,855 graduates and contributed an estimated Rs.115.5 billion to the Sri Lankan economy.\n\nAccording to the Economic Impact Study, NSBM\u2019s contribution during its first decade as a self-financed institution has extended well beyond its core role in higher education, encompassing human capital development, employment generation, foreign exchange retention, regional economic growth, sustainability and community development.\n\nThe findings underscore the significant and growing national and regional footprint of NSBM over the past decade, with the university\u2019s operations, graduates and wider ecosystem contributing substantially to economic value creation.\n\nThe assessment demonstrates that NSBM\u2019s impact extends well beyond the university campus, generating value through skilled human capital, retaining foreign exchange through local higher education, transforming the regional economy, building a sustainable university model and developing community standards.\n\nBeyond education: NSBM\u2019s growing contribution to Sri Lanka\u2019s economy and human capital\n\nOver the past decade, NSBM Green University has evolved beyond its core role as a higher education provider to become an institution with a growing economic, social and regional footprint in Sri Lanka. To better understand and quantify this wider contribution, NSBM commissioned the Strategy, Risk and Transactions team of Deloitte FAS (Pvt.) Ltd along with subject matter expert, Prof. Priyanga Dunusinghe to undertake an economic impact assessment of the university, examining its contribution across areas including economic value creation, human capital development, employment, foreign exchange retention, regional development, sustainability and community impact."
  },
  {
