    "md5": "a0a1588f3f0449cf711c6f11c97317c0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1567,
    "chunk_text": "Beyond reducing outward foreign exchange expenditure, retaining students locally allows a greater share of household education expenditure to circulate within Sri Lanka through spending on education, accommodation, transport, food and other services.\n\nOne of the clearest illustrations of NSBM\u2019s wider impact can be seen in the area surrounding NSBM Green University. The establishment and continued growth of the university community in Pitipana have created sustained demand for accommodation, food, transport, retail and other services, supporting the development of a wider local economy around the campus.\n\nThe assessment estimates that student expenditure generates approximately Rs.4.2 billion in annual value creation within the Homagama area, while regional economic activity associated with NSBM supports more than 22,000 livelihoods.\n\nOf the local businesses surveyed as part of the assessment, 67 percent were established after NSBM, demonstrating the growth in commercial activity that has taken place alongside the development of the university.\n\nChanges in the surrounding area are also visible through land values and patterns of development. The study identifies a real increase of more than 830 percent in land values since NSBM\u2019s establishment, while satellite-derived nighttime light data shows a substantial increase in light intensity around the campus between 2013 and 2026.\n\nTogether with the business survey findings, these indicators highlight the broader transformation of Homagama, Pitipana into an emerging education and services hub."
