    "md5": "a809643741e63fe4d23697d07c07ae9e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1773,
    "chunk_text": "Prime lending rate eases to 10.86% as banks diverge on pricing\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s benchmark prime lending rate edged down during the week ending August 21, 2026, breaking recent upward momentum even as individual commercial banks displayed contrasting pricing strategies for their prime corporate borrowers.\n\nAccording to data released by the Central Bank of Sri Lanka, the Average Weighted Prime Lending Rate (AWPR) declined by 9 basis points to 10.86 per cent, down from 10.95 per cent recorded in the preceding week. Despite the slight week-on-week easing, prime borrowing costs remain up 279 basis points from 8.07 per cent a year earlier, reflecting cumulative monetary adjustments and broader market rate realignments over the past year.\n\nA granular breakdown of bank-wise lending rates revealed divergent trends across domestic private, state, and foreign commercial banks. Among state-owned lenders, the country\u2019s largest bank, Bank of Ceylon, trimmed its prime lending rate by 17 basis points to 10.89 percent from 11.06 percent. In contrast, People\u2019s Bank raised its prime rate by an equivalent 17 basis points to 11.02 percent from 10.85 percent a week earlier.\n\nKey domestic private commercial banks predominantly experienced modest upward adjustments during the week. Commercial Bank of Ceylon saw its AWPR increase to 11.27 percent from 11.02 percent, while Hatton National Bank climbed to 11.36 percent from 11.18 percent. Sampath Bank and Seylan Bank recorded rates of 11.45 per cent and 11.39 percent, respectively, both firming from 11.25 percent in the previous week.\n\nBucking the upward trend among private peers, Union Bank of Colombo posted a substantial rate reduction of 131 basis points, bringing its AWPR down to 12.31 per cent from 13.62 percent."
