    "chunk_text": "Key domestic private commercial banks predominantly experienced modest upward adjustments during the week. Commercial Bank of Ceylon saw its AWPR increase to 11.27 percent from 11.02 percent, while Hatton National Bank climbed to 11.36 percent from 11.18 percent. Sampath Bank and Seylan Bank recorded rates of 11.45 per cent and 11.39 percent, respectively, both firming from 11.25 percent in the previous week.\n\nBucking the upward trend among private peers, Union Bank of Colombo posted a substantial rate reduction of 131 basis points, bringing its AWPR down to 12.31 per cent from 13.62 percent.\n\nForeign banks operating in the country continued to anchor the lower band of prime lending rates, though the picture was complicated by thin trading among some lenders. Citi Bank recorded the lowest rate in the sector at 8.25 percent, though the Central Bank noted the bank had not extended fresh loans to prime customers during the week, meaning the figure reflects its last available transaction rather than current pricing \u2014 a caveat that also applies to Cargills Bank, MCB Bank and Bank of China this week.\n\nAmong banks with active pricing, State Bank of India offered the most competitive terms at 9.88 percent, followed by Deutsche Bank at 9.89 per cent. HSBC and Standard Chartered Bank eased their prime rates to 10.09 percent and 10.26 per cent, respectively, whereas Indian Bank recorded the highest rate in the banking system at 13.82 percent.\n\nThe AWPR\u2019s dip came against a backdrop of ample, if narrowing, systemic liquidity. Outstanding market liquidity stood at a surplus of Rs. 278.51 billion by August 21, down from Rs. 290.69 billion a week earlier."
