    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "eff1352a00abc5c01b2cf8ef29c60cb9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1565,
    "chunk_text": "As inflation bites, shoppers ditch brands for bargains\n\nSparkwinn\u2019s household panel shows spending on non-branded essentials rebounded to 57% by June, as consumers scaled back branded consumption to cope with rising costs\n\nHeadline inflation accelerated to 7.2% in July, driven by food inflation climbing to a multi-year high of 6.3%\n\nBudget brands and supermarket private labels may gain traction as consumers turn increasingly value-conscious, Sparkwinn\u2019s Perampalam says\n\nSri Lankan consumers are spending less on branded goods and more on loose, generic products as inflation rises, according to the latest household panel data from Sparkwinn Research.\n\nThe shift comes after a year in which branded spending had been recovering, following a period of deflation that set in after the 2022 economic crisis, when inflation topped 70 percent.\n\nThe panel shows branded goods\u2019 share of household spending rose from 38 percent in July 2025 to a peak of 49 percent between February and April 2026, a period of relatively low inflation. That share fell to 44 percent in May and 43 percent in June as inflation picked up, while the share of non-branded goods \u2014 loose, generic and unpackaged items such as vegetables, fruit and meat \u2014 rose to 57 percent.\n\nHeadline inflation rose from 5.4 percent in April to 7.2 percent in July. Food inflation, which hits households more directly, reached a multi-year high of 6.3 percent in July. Wages haven\u2019t kept pace, and the price increases have pushed consumers to prioritise necessities over discretionary purchases."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-24-dailymirrorlk-d7fd6d98",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-d7fd6d98",
    "date_str": "2026-08-24",
    "description": "As inflation bites, shoppers ditch brands for bargains",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-main/As-inflation-bites-shoppers-ditch-brands-for-bargains/245-348725",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787510191.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-24-dailymirrorlk-d7fd6d98-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "43aab698eb10d7b0af309adebed58790",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "Headline inflation rose from 5.4 percent in April to 7.2 percent in July. Food inflation, which hits households more directly, reached a multi-year high of 6.3 percent in July. Wages haven\u2019t kept pace, and the price increases have pushed consumers to prioritise necessities over discretionary purchases.\n\n\u201cOver the past two to three quarters, our household panel had shown a gradual increase in consumer spending on branded goods. However, as inflation gathers momentum, household budgets are likely to come under renewed pressure, prompting consumers to seek more affordable and value-driven ways to meet their everyday needs,\u201d Sparkwinn Research Managing Director Suthaharan Perampalam told Mirror Business.\n\nSparkwinn has been running the household panel for the past 14 months, tracking consumer spending on branded and non-branded goods among a selected panel of respondents through a diary-based approach.\n\nThe shift is already showing up in corporate earnings. Among listed companies with FMCG operations, the slowdown in demand has been most evident in non-essential, discretionary categories such as homecare, where households scaled back consumption during the June quarter to protect budgets for essentials.\n\nDespite the contraction in branded goods\u2019 share, Perampalam cautioned against reading the trend as a wholesale rejection of brands, describing it instead as a strategic budgeting response by households.\n\n\u201cAt the outset, it may appear that a fraction of consumers are moving away from brands, but what we are seeing is a declining trend in spending on branded goods,\u201d he said. \u201cThis could be an early indication, potentially linked to rising inflation. Consumers may not be rejecting brands; they are managing branded consumption to stay within tighter budgets.\n\nThey may trade down, buy smaller packs, purchase less frequently, choosing cheaper ones over premium, or wait for promotions, while continuing to choose trusted brands selectively.\u201d"
  },
