    "md5": "43e24a7e9aedb8e47145043e6ddf4536",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1625,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cAt the outset, it may appear that a fraction of consumers are moving away from brands, but what we are seeing is a declining trend in spending on branded goods,\u201d he said. \u201cThis could be an early indication, potentially linked to rising inflation. Consumers may not be rejecting brands; they are managing branded consumption to stay within tighter budgets.\n\nThey may trade down, buy smaller packs, purchase less frequently, choosing cheaper ones over premium, or wait for promotions, while continuing to choose trusted brands selectively.\u201d\n\n\u201cFor brands, the opportunity is to protect affordability through pack-size options, value packs, targeted promotions and clear value propositions, rather than relying solely on price cuts,\u201d he said.\n\nLooking ahead, Perampalam said budget brands and supermarket private labels could gain further ground as household budgets stay stretched.\n\n\u201cAs inflation rises and household wallets come under pressure, budget brands and supermarket private labels may gain traction as consumers become increasingly value-conscious,\u201d he said. \u201cWhere the price differential is meaningful, consumers may be more willing to consider supermarket-own label alternatives, particularly in frequently purchased, everyday categories.\u201d\n\nHe added that the shift could reshape competition between retailers and established brands.\n\n\u201cThis could create an opportunity for supermarkets to strengthen their own-label propositions, while established brands may need to reinforce their value proposition by balancing affordability with the quality and trust that underpin brand choice,\u201d he said."
