    "md5": "c632b7d980e8c1a195965db99d41def0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1701,
    "chunk_text": "The controversial 22nd Amendment to the Constitution\n\nThe much talked of 22nd Amendment to the Constitution was tabled in Parliament on\u00a0 August 18 2026 by the Minister of Justice. According to the government, the bill itself is designed to roll back the concentration of unchecked executive powers granted under the 20th Amendment (during the presidency of Gotabaya Rajapaksa) and restore key parliamentary checks and balances.\n\nThe 22nd Amendment to the Constitution speaks of Trimming Executive Presidential Powers. Under the proposed Amendment,the President can no longer arbitrarily remove the Prime Minister or the Cabinet. The President must act on the Prime Minister\u2019s advice when appointing or dismissing Ministers.\n\nIt bars Dual Citizens, (individuals holding dual citizenship) from contesting elections or holding public office (e.g., as MPs or President).\n\nIt seeks to re-establish the Constitutional Council to oversee key state bodies and appointments (such as the Elections Commission, Bribery Commission, Human Rights Commission, and Police Commission).\n\nIt places the appointment of the Governor of the Central Bank of Sri Lanka under the approval of the Constitutional Council and Strengthens Anti-Corruption Safeguards via enhanced financial and constitutional oversight to enforce greater government accountability.\n\nHowever what comes across very loudly are voices from the parliamentary Opposition, saying that the bill is all about the extension of dates for the retirement age of judges in the country. The parliamentary Opposition tells all of us citizens\u2019 of Lanka, that the government simply wants to lengthen the tenure of the present Chief Justice (CJ) of the country."
  },
