    "md5": "59fd0d4d4c090cb94087c20d275737dc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "JR, Judiciary and Political Elimination of Sirimavo\n\nIn August 1978, a Special Presidential Commission comprising Supreme Court judges Weeraratne and Sharvananda and High Court judge K.C.E. de Alwis was appointed to investigate events between May 1970 and July 1977, when Bandaranaike had been\u00a0Prime Minister\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Jayewardene era contains a troubling chapter in which constitutional power and State machinery were deployed against rival Sirimavo Bandaranaike.\n\nIf Jayewardene was determined to remove Bandaranaike from political life, it was not because he regarded her as incapable. On the contrary, he had acknowledged her abilities and achievements. In 1972, while Opposition leader and Deputy Leader of the UNP, he even suggested that he might leave the party with supporters and join her government if the UNP rejected an invitation to participate. He publicly conceded that Bandaranaike had introduced more socialist reforms during her seven years as Prime Minister than previous leaders had achieved.\n\nThe remarks brought criticism within the UNP. In February 1972, its Working Committee appointed a five-member committee to inquire into his conduct, but nothing came of it. After Dudley Senanayake\u2019s death in April 1973, Jayewardene became UNP leader.\n\nHis attitude towards Bandaranaike later changed decisively. He understood the electorate\u2019s volatility and feared that, when the political pendulum eventually swung against his government, a charismatic Bandaranaike could become the opposition\u2019s rallying point. The objective went beyond defeating her at an election: to make her legally incapable of contesting or campaigning for another opponent.\n\nJayewardene personally oversaw the programme. In February 1978, the Special Presidential Commissions Law No. 7 of 1978 was enacted. It empowered the President to appoint commissions to inquire into alleged political victimisation, misuse or abuse of power, corruption and fraud, and to recommend civic disability."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-24-dailymirrorlk-bfde4a4b",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-bfde4a4b",
    "date_str": "2026-08-24",
    "description": "JR, Judiciary and Political Elimination of Sirimavo",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/JR-Judiciary-and-Political-Elimination-of-Sirimavo/172-348729",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787512240.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-24-dailymirrorlk-bfde4a4b-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8f01cdc0d30afa577825e855cfb68b2d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1917,
    "chunk_text": "Jayewardene personally oversaw the programme. In February 1978, the Special Presidential Commissions Law No. 7 of 1978 was enacted. It empowered the President to appoint commissions to inquire into alleged political victimisation, misuse or abuse of power, corruption and fraud, and to recommend civic disability.\n\nThe appointment was followed by an extraordinary restructuring of the Supreme Court. Twelve senior serving judges were removed from their previous positions; four were demoted to the Appeal Court and eight left out altogether. Weeraratne rose from eleventh to fifth in seniority and Sharvananda from fourteenth to sixth. De Alwis was elevated over 18 High Court judges to the Appeal Court. The changes caused concern in legal circles.\n\nWhen the commission sought to investigate Bandaranaike, she challenged its jurisdiction before the Appeal Court. On November 9, 1978, the court ruled that the commission could not inquire into her 1970-77 administration because those events predated the law establishing the commission. A writ of prohibition followed.\n\nThe Special Presidential Commissions of Inquiry (Special Provisions) Act No. 4 of 1978 declared the court judgment null and void and gave retrospective effect to the allegations against Bandaranaike. A constitutional amendment passed on November 20 authorised Parliament to remove jurisdiction in specified categories from the Appeal Court and confer it on the Supreme Court, also retrospectively.\n\nThe message was clear: The courts would not obstruct his political programme.\n\nThere were, however, voices of opposition. During the constitutional debate on August 3, 1978, an SLFP speaker warned that the establishment of what was described as the \u201cautocracy of JR Jayewardene\u201d would be met by a struggle outside Parliament. The warning anticipated State power, arrests and draconian laws, but insisted that the SLFP would not be intimidated."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-24-dailymirrorlk-bfde4a4b",
