    "chunk_text": "On October 16, Parliament passed two resolutions. One deprived Bandaranaike of her civic rights for seven years; the other expelled her from Parliament. Election laws were then amended to prevent a person under such disability from speaking, canvassing or acting as an agent at parliamentary elections. Violations could attract imprisonment or a fine, while candidates at whose instance the disqualified person participated could themselves be disqualified. Similar amendments were made to the Presidential Election Act.\n\nThe parliamentary episode was painful. Opposition Leader A. Amirthalingam argued that the voters had already given their verdict by returning Bandaranaike to Parliament and that Parliament had no right retrospectively to sit in judgment on that electoral decision. He described the creation of retrospective offences and punishment as inconsistent with fundamental rights and international human-rights principles.\n\nBandaranaike then delivered a brief farewell speech. As she left her seat amid indecent remarks and catcalls from government benches, the other six SLFP MPs boycotted the sitting. She was about to leave the chamber unaccompanied when Amirthalingam and Sivasithamparam rose and escorted her out, a striking display of parliamentary courtesy amid political humiliation. The campaign continued. In August 1981, Jayewardene used the Public Security Ordinance to take possession and control of the SLFP headquarters as preparations intensified for the elections expected in 1983.\n\nLong afterwards, Buddhist monks led by the late Kosgoda Dhammawansa appealed to Jayewardene to restore Bandaranaike\u2019s civic rights. He recalled his son\u2019s detention during the 1971 insurgency. Asked whether his actions were revenge, he did not answer. Yet the strategy was larger than personal retaliation: it was aimed at preventing a powerful rival from returning to the political battlefield."
