    "md5": "e6a14066388ec3b420b6c50654b9379c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1733,
    "chunk_text": "Prof. Weddikkara: Technology should support our profession, not define it. The experience, judgement, ethics and wisdom of a Quantity Surveyor remain fundamental to good professional practice. The Congress gives us an opportunity to embrace technological advancement while preserving and strengthening the human intelligence that sits at the heart of the profession.\n\nQ: How does Sri Lanka\u2019s participation in PAQS benefit professionals and the domestic industry?\n\nSeneviratne: When new procurement methods or contract standards emerge, or when we face problems, we can tap into other member countries and see how they have solved those problems. Sharing lessons learned from other countries is very valuable. At the same time, Sri Lankan professionals who gain experience overseas can potentially return when the local economy requires their expertise and contribute significantly to the construction industry and the wider economy.\n\nQ: Sri Lankan Quantity Surveyors are highly regarded internationally. What drives that reputation?\n\nSeneviratne: One of the reasons is our professional and ethical standards. Quantity Surveyors manage other people's money and clients' money, so one has to be very ethical in doing this. We have produced a strong brand of Sri Lankan Quantity Surveyors who are capable of doing this successfully.\n\nProf. Weddikkara: I would add sound technical knowledge, practical experience, adaptability and professional integrity. Sri Lankan professionals have been exposed to a wide range of projects and challenging commercial environments. Many have progressed into senior management and leadership positions internationally, reflecting the confidence placed in their professional judgement and capabilities."
  },
