    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6c6b88c1fdc7bd4d5c7787dc330ec029",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1849,
    "chunk_text": "NCE to lead exporters\u2019 delegation to India to unlock new opportunities\n\nInvites Lankan exporters to join business delegation to New Delhi and Mumbai from 24 September to 1 October 2026\n\nThe National Chamber of Exporters of Sri Lanka (NCE) is inviting exporters, manufacturers, aspiring exporters and other businesses interested in expanding into the Indian market to join an overseas exporters\u2019 delegation to New Delhi and Mumbai from 24 September to 1 October 2026.\n\nIndia is one of Sri Lanka\u2019s most important trading partners and presents significant opportunities for expanding Sri Lankan exports. In 2025, Sri Lanka\u2019s exports to India increased by 17.2% to approximately $1.04 billion, compared with $881.6 million in 2024. The positive momentum has continued in 2026, with exports to India increasing by 36.16% to $688.47 million during the first six months of the year.\n\nWith a population exceeding 1.4 billion and a rapidly expanding consumer and business economy, India offers considerable opportunities for Sri Lankan companies across multiple sectors. These include tea, rubber and rubber based products, coconut products, spices, apparel and textiles, food and beverages, gems and jewellery, electrical and electronic products, ICT and BPM services, logistics, manufacturing and other value-added products and services.\n\nNCE said there are also opportunities beyond traditional exports, including joint ventures, investment, contract manufacturing, distribution partnerships, technology collaboration and integration into Indian and regional supply chains.\n\nSri Lankan exporters have the advantage of the India Sri Lanka Free Trade Agreement (ISFTA), which has provided preferential market access since 2000. In 2025, approximately $634 million, or 61.3% of Sri Lanka\u2019s exports to India, were exported under the ISFTA framework."
  },
  {
