    "md5": "e555818e0a957ffdb34fbd92be005303",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Life in place of death? New plan heads to Cabinet\n\nColombo, Aug. 24 (Daily Mirror) - Despite renewed attention to capital punishment at a high-level anti-narcotics meeting chaired by President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, the government is now preparing to submit a Cabinet paper proposing a formal framework that would include commuting death sentences to life imprisonment, reducing life sentences to 20 years and allowing home leave for eligible prisoners.\n\nA formal framework for granting presidential pardons to prisoners serving death or life sentences is expected to be submitted to the Cabinet shortly, Minister of Justice and National Integration Harshana Nanayakkara said.\n\nThe Minister made the remarks during an inspection of the Colombo Remand Prison, New Magazine Prison and Welikada Prison.\n\nHe said a committee headed by retired Supreme Court Judge S. Thurairaja completed a 10-month study on establishing a structured procedure for presidential pardons and submitted its report.\n\nAccording to Nanayakkara, the committee recommended establishing a formal presidential pardon procedure, commuting death sentences to life imprisonment, reducing life sentences to 20 years and introducing home leave for eligible prisoners.\n\nThe Minister said the recommendations would be submitted to the Cabinet of Ministers without delay.\n\nEarlier this month, Prison authorities said 806 inmates are currently on death row, including 58 convicted in drug-related cases. Of those convicted in drug-related cases, 52 are male and six are female. One foreign national is also among those on death row.\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake recently highlighted capital punishment during a high-level anti-narcotics meeting, where officials discussed the possibility of imposing the death penalty for major drug offences. However, the government later clarified that no final policy decision or official agreement had been reached to end the country\u2019s long-standing moratorium on executions."
  },
