    "chunk_size_bytes": 1302,
    "chunk_text": "The PUCSL has also imposed a cost ceiling on BESS capacity already offered to the system. The NSO has been instructed to ensure that the average monthly cost of the 160MW of BESS already offered does not exceed Rs. 20 per kWh.\n\nAny cost exceeding the Rs. 20 per kWh threshold will not be recognised, recovered or permitted to be passed on to electricity consumers through the end-user tariff.\n\nThe revised tariff structure also covers mini-hydro, wind, biomass, municipal solid waste, waste heat recovery, ground-mounted solar PV and floating solar PV projects.\n\nPUCSL said the tariff methodology was approved under Section 29 of the Sri Lanka Electricity Act No. 36 of 2024 following a public consultation process. Stakeholder submissions and project cost data were considered in determining the final rates.\n\nThe introduction of higher tariffs for solar-plus-storage projects signals a shift towards using battery systems to store renewable electricity and make it available when demand is high, particularly during periods when additional generation capacity is required.\n\nWith the NSO targeting 450MW of solar PV with BESS by March 2027, the latest tariff decision is expected to play a key role in efforts to strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s electricity supply while expanding renewable energy generation."
