    "chunk_size_bytes": 1950,
    "chunk_text": "Are our banks losing credibility and public confidence?\n\nColombo, Aug. 24 (Daily Mirror) - An alleged $1 billion money-laundering scheme executed through \u201cphantom imports\u201d has shattered confidence in Sri Lanka\u2019s banking sector. With 13 banks under investigation for processing thousands of fraudulent transactions, the scandal exposes severe breakdowns in basic Know Your Customer protocols and real-time Customs integration. Uncovering this vast network of shell companies proves that compliance cannot remain a mere paper-pushing exercise. To prevent catastrophic capital flight, Sri Lanka must radically transform its financial governance into an active, intelligence-driven defensive shield.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s banking sector is facing a defining test of credibility as investigations into an alleged large-scale foreign-exchange and money-laundering operation reveal how vast sums of money could allegedly be transferred overseas through the banking system under the guise of imports that never arrived.\n\nThe issue is no longer simply about individual fraudsters or isolated weaknesses in internal controls. It raises a far more serious question. Can the public and international investors continue to have confidence in a banking system if billions of rupees can allegedly pass through commercial banks using fabricated import documentation, shell companies and thousands of telegraphic transfers without triggering effective intervention?\n\nAuthorities have disclosed investigations into an extensive network involving 105 shell companies, 227 bank accounts and more than 24,000\u201326,000 telegraphic transfers, with approximately US$715 million identified in one investigation. Separately, President Dissanayake has referred to an alleged outflow approaching US$1 billion through advance payments for imports that apparently never materialized. Investigators have also examined links to suspected money laundering and other criminal activity."
  },
