    "md5": "0e7a7f60612db768a39ec0115b0296ba",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1643,
    "chunk_text": "Authorities have disclosed investigations into an extensive network involving 105 shell companies, 227 bank accounts and more than 24,000\u201326,000 telegraphic transfers, with approximately US$715 million identified in one investigation. Separately, President Dissanayake has referred to an alleged outflow approaching US$1 billion through advance payments for imports that apparently never materialized. Investigators have also examined links to suspected money laundering and other criminal activity.\n\nInvestigating authorities have disclosed that 13 Banks in Sri Lanka were involved in processing transactions currently under investigation.\n\nThis raises an even more fundamental question. Were the most basic banking safeguards actually being applied?\n\nBanks have a fundamental obligation to conduct adequate Customer Due Diligence (CDD) and Know Your Customer (KYC) procedures. These are not optional administrative formalities. They are among the most basic and paramount safeguards in the global banking system against fraud, money laundering, illicit transfers and the misuse of financial institutions.\n\nYet investigators have identified instances where KYC is not followed and accounts were allegedly opened without the named account holder being physically present.\n\nThere are also reports of accounts being closed at one branch and subsequently reopened at another, in circumstances where officers moved between branches over the years. How could a customer\u2019s banking history and risk profile be effectively monitored if accounts could effectively move from one branch environment to another without adequate institutional scrutiny?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
