    "md5": "a12788f6657521058123aa1d32e8fc34",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1896,
    "chunk_text": "One of the clearest weaknesses exposed by the investigations is the absence, or previous inadequacy, of real-time connectivity between banking systems and the Customs/import-monitoring infrastructure.\n\nThat gap effectively created an opportunity for money to leave the country before the system could establish whether the promised goods ever arrived.\n\nEvery import-related outward remittance should generate a digital transaction trail from the bank to Customs and back to the bank. If an importer receives approval to remit US dollars for a shipment, the system should automatically monitor whether the shipment arrives. If it does not arrive within the prescribed period, the transaction should automatically be flagged, escalated and subjected to investigation.\n\nThough the Government has already moved towards tighter controls, including requirements for real-time reporting of import-related foreign-currency payments and registration of importers with Customs. But sadly, it appears these are remaining merely regulatory requirements on paper. Unless these measures are implemented comprehensively there is a risk of repeat frauds.\n\nSri Lanka needs a zero-tolerance banking integrity framework built on real-time bank\u2013Customs data integration, automated monitoring of suspicious transactions, enhanced due diligence, independent approval of high-value remittances, regular rotation of high-risk banking staff, protected whistle-blower channels, forensic audits, strong board-level oversight, personal accountability for collusion or bribery, and intelligence-sharing across banks. The objective must be simple. Prevent fraud before money leaves the country and ensure that no customer or bank employee can exploit regulatory gaps with impunity. Most importantly, the banking industry must stop treating compliance as a paperwork exercise. Compliance must become an intelligence function."
  },
